Guide.content._register("w09", {"id":"w09","order":9,"region":"agents","title":"A2A and Interop","short":"A2A and interop","tagline":"When the work belongs to another agent, treat it as a peer, not a function.","story":"The work belongs to someone else. Another team, vendor or framework owns the agent that can finish it, and you cannot see its prompts, tools or memory. Here the traps are treating a peer like a function call, blocking on work that takes an hour, and trusting an Agent Card that anyone could have edited.","oneLiner":"Learn how the Agent2Agent protocol lets opaque agents discover each other, run tasks and exchange artifacts, and when an in-process tool, an MCP server or an A2A peer is the right boundary.","youWillLearn":["Why A2A exists, what opaque execution means, and how A2A differs from MCP and from agent-as-tool","How to read an Agent Card: interfaces, skills, capabilities, security schemes, signing and caching","How Message, Part, Task and Artifact fit together, and how the task lifecycle behaves, including input-required and auth-required","How to choose between polling, streaming and push notifications, and how to secure webhooks","How the three bindings, version negotiation and the 0.3 to 1.0 migration work","How authentication, authorization and trust work between agents you do not own","How Microsoft Agent Framework and Foundry expose and call A2A endpoints, and their limits","How to defend an interop design for agents owned by different teams or vendors"],"minutes":108,"prereqs":["w08"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w09-fc-001","front":"What does A2A solve that MCP does not?","back":"MCP gives one agent tools and resources (vertical). A2A lets independent, often opaque agents discover each other, share tasks and exchange results as peers (horizontal) without exposing internals.","tags":["fundamentals"]},{"id":"w09-fc-002","front":"What does **opaque execution** mean in A2A?","back":"Agents collaborate through declared capabilities and exchanged messages. They do not share internal thoughts, plans, memory or tool implementations with each other.","tags":["fundamentals"]},{"id":"w09-fc-003","front":"Where is an Agent Card served and which fields are required?","back":"At `/.well-known/agent-card.json`. Required: `name`, `description`, `supportedInterfaces`, `version`, `capabilities`, `defaultInputModes`, `defaultOutputModes`, `skills`.","tags":["agent-card"]},{"id":"w09-fc-004","front":"What does each entry of `supportedInterfaces` hold?","back":"A `url`, a `protocolBinding` (`JSONRPC`, `GRPC` or `HTTP+JSON`), a `protocolVersion` and an optional `tenant`. The list is ordered and the first entry is preferred.","tags":["agent-card"]},{"id":"w09-fc-005","front":"Which three protocol bindings does A2A define?","back":"JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP, gRPC and HTTP+JSON. They must be functionally equivalent. Streaming uses SSE for JSON-RPC and HTTP+JSON, and native streams for gRPC.","tags":["bindings"]},{"id":"w09-fc-006","front":"Which A2A task states are terminal and which are interrupted?","back":"Terminal: `COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED`, `REJECTED`. Interrupted: `INPUT_REQUIRED` and `AUTH_REQUIRED`. Also `SUBMITTED`, `WORKING` and `UNSPECIFIED`.","tags":["tasks"]},{"id":"w09-fc-007","front":"Who generates `taskId` and `contextId`?","back":"The server. Clients cannot choose a new `taskId`. A message that carries both ids must have matching ones or the agent rejects it. Treat `contextId` as opaque.","tags":["tasks"]},{"id":"w09-fc-008","front":"What can `SendMessage` return?","back":"Either a Task that tracks the work or a direct Message for a simple interaction. Clients must handle both result shapes.","tags":["operations"]},{"id":"w09-fc-009","front":"Is `SendMessage` blocking by default?","back":"The configuration field `returnImmediately` defaults to false, so the call waits for a terminal or interrupted state. Set it true to get the task back at once and then poll, stream or use push.","tags":["operations"]},{"id":"w09-fc-010","front":"What is a **Part** in v1.0?","back":"The smallest content unit. It holds exactly one of `text`, `raw`, `url` or `data`, plus optional `mediaType`, `filename` and `metadata`. The v0.3 `kind` discriminator is gone.","tags":["objects"]},{"id":"w09-fc-011","front":"How do a Message and an Artifact differ?","back":"A Message is one communication turn with a role (`ROLE_USER` or `ROLE_AGENT`) and parts. An Artifact is a task output with an `artifactId` and parts.","tags":["objects"]},{"id":"w09-fc-012","front":"What happens to a task after it reaches a terminal state?","back":"It cannot restart or take more messages. A refinement starts a new task in the same `contextId`, optionally with `referenceTaskIds`.","tags":["tasks"]},{"id":"w09-fc-013","front":"Name the three ways a client gets task updates.","back":"Polling with `GetTask`, streaming with `SendStreamingMessage` or `SubscribeToTask` (needs `capabilities.streaming`), and webhook push (needs `capabilities.pushNotifications`).","tags":["delivery"]},{"id":"w09-fc-014","front":"Which webhook defenses does A2A require or recommend?","back":"Agents SHOULD reject private, loopback and link-local URLs and use 10 to 30 second timeouts with backoff. Receivers MUST verify credentials, reply 2xx, check the task id and SHOULD process idempotently.","tags":["delivery","security"]},{"id":"w09-fc-015","front":"How do clients pick an A2A version?","back":"They send `A2A-Version: Major.Minor` on each request. An empty header means 0.3. An unsupported version returns `VersionNotSupportedError`. Patch numbers never affect compatibility.","tags":["versioning"]},{"id":"w09-fc-016","front":"Where does identity live in A2A?","back":"At the HTTP layer, not in the payload. Every request MUST be authenticated, credentials come out of band and TLS is mandatory. Schemes are declared in the card's `securitySchemes`.","tags":["auth"]},{"id":"w09-fc-017","front":"What is `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` for?","back":"In-task authorization. The agent hands the client the job of getting a credential, ideally out of band. The state alone authorizes nothing, and a credential does not cover later messages unless defined.","tags":["auth","tasks"]},{"id":"w09-fc-018","front":"How is an Agent Card signed and cached?","back":"Optionally with a JWS (RFC 7515) over the RFC 8785 canonical form, excluding `signatures`. Servers SHOULD send `Cache-Control: max-age` and `ETag`. Clients SHOULD use conditional requests.","tags":["agent-card"]},{"id":"w09-fc-019","front":"What is the status and timeline of A2A v1.0?","back":"v1.0.0 was released on 2026-03-12 and v1.0.1, a patch, on 2026-05-28. Google created A2A in April 2025 and the Linux Foundation launched the project on 2025-06-23.","tags":["fundamentals"]},{"id":"w09-fc-020","front":"Foundry `a2a` versus `a2a_preview` tool types?","back":"`a2a` targets A2A 1.0 and is generally available. `a2a_preview` targets 0.3 and is in preview. Use `a2a` for new integrations. The classic Connected Agents tool is not in the new Foundry Agent Service.","tags":["microsoft"]},{"id":"w09-fc-021","front":"What are the limits of incoming A2A on a Foundry agent?","back":"v1.0 over JSON-RPC only, text only, no streaming, needs the responses protocol, Entra auth with the Foundry Agent Consumer role. Tasks and contexts are kept 60 days from last write. No version selector means 0.3.","tags":["microsoft"]},{"id":"w09-fc-022","front":"How does Agent Framework host and call A2A agents?","back":".NET hosts with `MapA2AHttpJson` and calls with `A2AAgent`. Python uses `A2AExecutor` and `A2AAgent` from `agent-framework-a2a`. Packages are prerelease. The A2A `context_id` becomes the session id.","tags":["microsoft"]},{"id":"w09-fc-023","front":"When should you use agent-as-tool instead of A2A?","back":"When agents share a process and a team. A2A adds value across service, team or organization boundaries, at the cost of network latency, retries, timeouts and versioning.","tags":["fundamentals","microsoft"]},{"id":"w09-t01-fc1","front":"What problem does A2A solve that wrapping an agent as a tool does not?","back":"It exposes agents as they are, with tasks, context, artifacts and discovery, instead of reducing them to a function signature.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t01"},{"id":"w09-t01-fc2","front":"What does a caller see of a remote A2A agent?","back":"The Agent Card plus the messages, tasks and artifacts exchanged. Prompts, tools and memory stay hidden.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t01"},{"id":"w09-t01-fc3","front":"When was A2A v1.0.0 released, and who governs the project?","back":"2026-03-12. The Linux Foundation launched the A2A project on 2025-06-23.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t01"},{"id":"w09-t02-fc1","front":"How does the A2A project describe the MCP and A2A split?","back":"MCP is vertical (an agent to its tools and resources). A2A is horizontal (independent agents to each other). Use A2A between agents and MCP inside each.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t02"},{"id":"w09-t02-fc2","front":"When is an in-process agent tool the right call?","back":"When one team ships both agents in one process. A2A adds network latency, retries and versioning that only pay off across boundaries.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t02"},{"id":"w09-t02-fc3","front":"What does a plain MCP tool call lack that an A2A task has?","back":"A task lifecycle: ids, states, progress, input-required turns and artifacts, unless the MCP Tasks extension is used.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t02"},{"id":"w09-t03-fc1","front":"Where does an A2A server publish its Agent Card by default?","back":"At `/.well-known/agent-card.json` on its domain. Foundry agents use an authenticated custom path, `agentCard/v1.0`.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t03"},{"id":"w09-t03-fc2","front":"Which Agent Card fields tell a client which operations are available?","back":"`capabilities` (`streaming`, `pushNotifications`, `extensions`, `extendedAgentCard`). A missing flag means the operation returns `UnsupportedOperationError`.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t03"},{"id":"w09-t03-fc3","front":"How is an Agent Card signed?","back":"As a JWS (RFC 7515) over the RFC 8785 canonical form of the card, excluding the `signatures` field.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t03"},{"id":"w09-t03-fc4","front":"Where should sensitive card detail live?","back":"In the authenticated extended card (`GetExtendedAgentCard`), not the public card.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t03"},{"id":"w09-t04-fc1","front":"Name the four terminal and two interrupted A2A task states.","back":"Terminal: `COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED`, `REJECTED`. Interrupted: `INPUT_REQUIRED`, `AUTH_REQUIRED`.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t04"},{"id":"w09-t04-fc2","front":"How do you refine the result of a completed A2A task?","back":"Start a new task in the same `contextId`, optionally with `referenceTaskIds` pointing at the earlier task. Terminal tasks cannot restart.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t04"},{"id":"w09-t04-fc3","front":"Who generates `taskId` and `contextId`?","back":"The server. Clients echo them on follow-ups and cannot choose a new `taskId`. Mismatched ids are rejected.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t04"},{"id":"w09-t05-fc1","front":"What is the difference between a Message and an Artifact in A2A?","back":"Messages carry communication: questions, scoping and status. Artifacts carry task deliverables. Results SHOULD be artifacts.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t05"},{"id":"w09-t05-fc2","front":"Which four content fields can a Part use?","back":"Exactly one of `text`, `raw`, `url` or `data`, plus `mediaType`, `filename` and `metadata`.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t05"},{"id":"w09-t05-fc3","front":"What do `append` and `lastChunk` do on an artifact update?","back":"`append` extends an earlier artifact with the same id. `lastChunk` marks the final chunk, so large artifacts can stream.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t05"},{"id":"w09-t06-fc1","front":"Which three bindings does A2A v1.0 define?","back":"JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP, gRPC and HTTP+JSON. All must be functionally equivalent.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t06"},{"id":"w09-t06-fc2","front":"Which binding does a Foundry agent accept for incoming A2A v1.0?","back":"JSON-RPC only, with text modality and no streaming (checked 2026-10-13).","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t06"},{"id":"w09-t06-fc3","front":"What do an empty `A2A-Version` header and an unsupported version mean?","back":"Empty means 0.3. An unsupported version returns `VersionNotSupportedError`.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t06"},{"id":"w09-t07-fc1","front":"What does `SendMessageConfiguration.returnImmediately` do?","back":"If true, the task is returned right after creation. If false (default), the call waits until the task is terminal or interrupted.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t07"},{"id":"w09-t07-fc2","front":"How do you resume a dropped A2A stream?","back":"Call `SubscribeToTask`. The stream begins with the current `Task` snapshot, then continues with ordered events.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t07"},{"id":"w09-t07-fc3","front":"Which capability flags gate streaming and push?","back":"`capabilities.streaming` and `capabilities.pushNotifications` on the Agent Card. Without them the operations are unsupported.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t07"},{"id":"w09-t08-fc1","front":"How does a client resume an `INPUT_REQUIRED` task?","back":"By sending a new message with the same `taskId` and `contextId`. Mismatched ids are rejected.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t08"},{"id":"w09-t08-fc2","front":"What does `AUTH_REQUIRED` authorize by itself?","back":"Nothing. Agents MUST NOT treat the state transition as authorization. Scope and validity come from the implementation, issuer or an extension.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t08"},{"id":"w09-t08-fc3","front":"How long does Foundry retain A2A tasks and contexts?","back":"60 days from the most recent write. Each write resets the period (checked 2026-10-13).","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t08"},{"id":"w09-t08-fc4","front":"How does Agent Framework surface a long A2A task?","back":"As a continuation token when background responses are enabled. Use `poll_task` or resubscribe, and session state keeps `context_id`, `task_id` and `task_state`.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t08"},{"id":"w09-t09-fc1","front":"Why does A2A put no identity in the message payload?","back":"Identity is established at the transport layer with standard HTTP credentials, so payload fields can be forged and are never trusted for auth.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t09"},{"id":"w09-t09-fc2","front":"What does `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` authorize on its own?","back":"Nothing. It asks the client to obtain a credential, and scope and validity come from the implementation, issuer or an extension.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t09"},{"id":"w09-t09-fc3","front":"What does a caller need to reach a Foundry agent over A2A?","back":"A Microsoft Entra token and the Foundry Agent Consumer role (or a role granting endpoint access), assigned to the object ID. No keys or anonymous access.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t09"},{"id":"w09-t10-fc1","front":"Which Foundry A2A tool type is GA and for which protocol version?","back":"`a2a` for A2A 1.0 (GA). `a2a_preview` is for 0.3 and is Preview.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t10"},{"id":"w09-t10-fc2","front":"What are the limits of Foundry's incoming A2A for v1.0?","back":"JSON-RPC only, text modality only, no streaming, Entra auth only, tasks and contexts kept 60 days from last write.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t10"},{"id":"w09-t10-fc3","front":"How do you install the Agent Framework A2A packages?","back":"`--prerelease` for .NET packages and `--pre` for `agent-framework-a2a` in Python. They are prerelease as of 2026-10-13.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t10"},{"id":"w09-t10-fc4","front":"What does `A2AAgent` hide from you?","back":"The remote agent's tools and reasoning. It is a transport wrapper, so tool changes happen on the remote side.","tags":[],"topic":"w09-t10"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"A2A Protocol specification","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/","type":"spec","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Normative source for objects, operations, bindings, cards and security.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"What's new in A2A v1.0","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/whats-new-v1/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Migration list from 0.3 to 1.0, including the three-phase migration advice.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"A2A and MCP: detailed comparison","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/a2a-and-mcp/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"The official statement of how the two protocols fit together.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r4","title":"A2A releases","url":"https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/releases","type":"repo","publisher":"A2A Project (GitHub)","note":"Release dates for v1.0.0 and v1.0.1.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r5","title":"Life of a Task","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/life-of-a-task/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Message versus task, contexts, refinements and task immutability.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r6","title":"Agent Discovery in A2A","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/agent-discovery/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Well-known URI, registries and direct configuration, and how to protect cards.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r7","title":"Streaming and Asynchronous Operations","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/streaming-and-async/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"When to stream, when to push and how to secure webhooks with JWT and JWKS.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r8","title":"Enterprise-ready features of A2A","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/enterprise-ready/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"TLS, authentication, tracing, auditing and API management guidance.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r9","title":"Linux Foundation launches the Agent2Agent Protocol Project","url":"https://www.linuxfoundation.org/press/linux-foundation-launches-the-agent2agent-protocol-project-to-enable-secure-intelligent-communication-between-ai-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Linux Foundation","note":"Governance change on 2025-06-23 and the claim of more than 100 supporting companies.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"A2A Python tutorial","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/tutorials/python/1-introduction/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Study material: build an agent and a client step by step.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r11","title":"A2A: The Agent2Agent Protocol (DeepLearning.AI)","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/a2a-the-agent2agent-protocol","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Study material: wrap agents as A2A servers and orchestrate them. Whether it covers v1.0 was not verified on 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"Agent-to-Agent (A2A) in Agent Framework","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/journey/agent-to-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Service, team and organization boundaries, and the cost of crossing them.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"Agents as tools in Agent Framework","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/journey/agents-as-tools","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The in-process alternative to A2A.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"Host agents with A2A (Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/hosting/self-hosting/a2a/server","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"MapA2AHttpJson, A2AExecutor and the prerelease packages.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r15","title":"A2A agent service (Agent Framework client)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/integrations/by-component/agent-services/a2a","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"A2AAgent, the card resolver, streaming and background responses with continuation tokens.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r16","title":"Connect to an A2A agent endpoint from Foundry Agent Service","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/tools/agent-to-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The a2a (1.0, generally available) and a2a_preview (0.3, preview) tool types and connection auth types.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"Enable incoming A2A on a Foundry agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/enable-agent-to-agent-endpoint","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Limitations, version selection, retention and the required role.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"RFC 7515: JSON Web Signature","url":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7515","type":"spec","publisher":"IETF","note":"The signature format that Agent Card signing uses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"RFC 8785: JSON Canonicalization Scheme","url":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8785","type":"spec","publisher":"IETF","note":"The canonical form that is signed.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"MCP Tasks extension overview","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/extensions/tasks/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Compare MCP's opt-in task handles with A2A's built-in task lifecycle.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"MCP authorization, 2026-07-28","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Compare MCP's defined OAuth profile with A2A's decision to leave identity to HTTP.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"What is A2A?","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/what-is-a2a/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Why wrapping agents as tools falls short and what A2A solves.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Core concepts and components in A2A","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/key-concepts/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Actors, opaque remote agents, Part fields, artifacts and interaction mechanisms in one page.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"RFC 8615: Well-Known Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)","url":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8615","type":"spec","publisher":"IETF","note":"The pattern behind the /.well-known/agent-card.json location.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Python 2026 Significant Changes Guide (Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/support/upgrade/python-2026-significant-changes","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Dated breaking changes, including the python-1.4.0 migration of agent-framework-a2a to a2a-sdk v1.0.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"}],"glossary":[{"term":"A2A client","def":"An application or agent that sends requests to an A2A server on behalf of a user or another system."},{"term":"A2A server (remote agent)","def":"An agent that exposes an A2A endpoint, processes tasks and returns messages or artifacts."},{"term":"Opaque execution","def":"Agents collaborate without sharing internal reasoning, memory or tool implementations."},{"term":"Agent Card","def":"A JSON document served at `/.well-known/agent-card.json` that describes an agent's identity, interfaces, skills, capabilities and security schemes."},{"term":"Skill","def":"A descriptive entry in an Agent Card with `id`, `name`, `description` and `tags` that tells callers what the agent is likely to succeed at."},{"term":"Task","def":"The stateful unit of work in A2A, with an id, a context, a status, artifacts and optional history."},{"term":"Message","def":"One communication turn between client and server with a role and one or more parts."},{"term":"Part","def":"The smallest content unit. It carries exactly one of text, raw bytes, a URL or structured data."},{"term":"Artifact","def":"An output of a task, such as a document or data, made of parts and identified by an `artifactId`."},{"term":"contextId","def":"A server-generated identifier that groups related tasks and messages into one conversation."},{"term":"Interrupted state","def":"A task state, `INPUT_REQUIRED` or `AUTH_REQUIRED`, where the agent waits for the client before it can continue."},{"term":"Push notification","def":"A server-initiated HTTP POST to a client-registered webhook that reports a task update."},{"term":"Protocol binding","def":"How the A2A operations map onto a transport: JSON-RPC over HTTP, gRPC or HTTP+JSON."},{"term":"Extended Agent Card","def":"A richer card returned only to authenticated clients when `capabilities.extendedAgentCard` is true."},{"term":"A2A-Version","def":"The request header that carries the `Major.Minor` protocol version the client speaks."},{"term":"Tenant","def":"An opaque routing value in an interface entry that lets one endpoint host many agents."},{"term":"Agent as tool","def":"Wrapping an agent as a function tool of another agent in the same process. Simple and fast, but couples releases and trust."},{"term":"Message-only, task-generating and hybrid agents","def":"Three reply styles. Message-only agents always send messages, task-generating agents always create tasks, and hybrid agents use messages to scope work and a task for committed work."},{"term":"Task immutability","def":"A task in a terminal state cannot restart. Refinements start a new task in the same `contextId`, optionally with `referenceTaskIds`."},{"term":"`referenceTaskIds`","def":"A message field listing earlier tasks that give context for a follow-up or refinement."},{"term":"`returnImmediately`","def":"A `SendMessageConfiguration` flag. False (default) blocks until the task is terminal or interrupted. True returns the task at once."},{"term":"`SubscribeToTask`","def":"The operation that reattaches to a task's event stream. It begins with the current `Task` snapshot."},{"term":"`StreamResponse`","def":"The wrapper for streamed and pushed updates. It holds exactly one of `task`, `message`, `statusUpdate` or `artifactUpdate`."},{"term":"In-task authorization","def":"The `AUTH_REQUIRED` flow where an agent asks the client for a credential mid-task. The state itself authorizes nothing."},{"term":"Foundry Agent Consumer role","def":"The Foundry role that grants an identity least-privilege access to call agent endpoints, including incoming A2A, without permission to create or change agents."},{"term":"`a2a` tool (Foundry)","def":"The generally available Foundry tool type for calling a remote A2A 1.0 agent through a project connection. `a2a_preview` targets 0.3 and is Preview."}],"levels":[{"id":"w09-l01","order":1,"title":"The work belongs to someone else","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"A job needs an agent that another team, vendor or framework owns. You cannot inspect its prompts, tools or memory. Whether you call it as a function, as an MCP tool or as a peer depends on ownership, task shape and how much of its internals you control.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three boundaries, three protocols","level":2,"id":"three-boundaries-three-protocols"},{"t":"p","md":"**A2A** is an open protocol for communication between independent, often opaque agent systems. Its design goals include opaque execution, support for long-running and human-in-the-loop work, and several interaction modes: request and response, streaming and push notifications [[r1]]. Google created it in April 2025 and the Linux Foundation launched the A2A project on 2025-06-23, citing more than 100 supporting companies [[r9]]. Version 1.0.0 was released on 2026-03-12 and the patch 1.0.1 on 2026-05-28 [[r4]].\n\nThe official comparison draws the line by direction. **MCP is vertical**: it deepens one agent with tools and resources. **A2A is horizontal**: it connects agents across a boundary, such as another team, department or organization. Inside each agent you keep using MCP, and between agents you use A2A [[r3]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-l01-d1","title":"MCP inside an agent, A2A between agents","alt":"Agent A uses MCP to reach its own tools and uses A2A to talk to Agent B, which belongs to another team. Agent B uses MCP to reach its own tools.","src":"flowchart LR\n  UA[User] --> A[Agent A]\n  A -->|MCP| T1[Tools of A]\n  A -->|A2A| B[Agent B]\n  B -->|MCP| T2[Tools of B]","caption":"Agent B stays opaque. Agent A sees skills and results, not B's tools, prompts or memory [[r3]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"Three ways to use another agent","intro":"Wrap it as an in-process tool, put it behind MCP, or call it as an A2A peer.","options":[{"name":"Agent as an in-process tool","tag":"Same team","summary":"Wrap the other agent as a function tool inside one process.","pros":["Simplest to build and debug","No network hop and shared tracing"],"cons":["Couples release cycles, language and runtime","No protocol-level task lifecycle. You build your own"],"bestWhen":"Both agents live in one process and one team maintains them [[r13]].","avoidWhen":"Separate release cycles, owners or languages.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"in-process"},{"k":"Trust","v":"shared"}]},{"name":"Agent behind an MCP tool","tag":"Tool-shaped","summary":"Expose the agent as an MCP tool so any MCP host can call it.","pros":["Reuses MCP hosts, auth profile and gateways","Fits a stateless, well-defined request and result"],"cons":["A tool call has no task lifecycle unless both sides support the Tasks extension [[r20]]","Multi-turn state needs explicit handles"],"bestWhen":"The agent behaves like a function with a fixed input and output.","avoidWhen":"Work is multi-turn, long-running or produces artifacts that need tracking.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"network"},{"k":"Trust","v":"OAuth profile"}]},{"name":"A2A peer","tag":"v1.0 GA","summary":"Discover the agent through its card and exchange messages and tasks.","pros":["Cards for discovery, a task lifecycle, input-required, artifacts","Streaming, push, versioning and signing are in the spec","The remote agent stays opaque"],"cons":["Network latency, retries, timeouts and version management","Platform support varies. Foundry incoming A2A is JSON-RPC, text only, with no streaming [[r17]]"],"bestWhen":"You cross a service, team or organization boundary [[r12]].","avoidWhen":"A shared process and team make a function call enough.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"network"},{"k":"Trust","v":"per-request auth"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w09-l01-boundary","title":"Pick the boundary","prompt":"Flip who owns the other agent, the shape of the work and how much of its internals you control.","dials":[{"id":"boundary","label":"Who owns the other agent","values":[{"id":"same","label":"Same team and process"},{"id":"org","label":"Another team in your company"},{"id":"other","label":"Another organization"}]},{"id":"shape","label":"Shape of the work","values":[{"id":"quick","label":"One quick answer"},{"id":"long","label":"Long-running, input needed, artifacts"}]},{"id":"internals","label":"Control over its internals","values":[{"id":"own","label":"You can inspect and change it"},{"id":"opaque","label":"Opaque, owned by others"}]}],"options":[{"id":"inproc","name":"In-process tool","summary":"A function wrapper inside one process."},{"id":"mcp","name":"MCP tool","summary":"The agent exposed as a tool."},{"id":"a2a","name":"A2A peer","summary":"Card, tasks, messages and artifacts."},{"id":"api","name":"Plain API","summary":"A deterministic service call with no agent."}],"scores":{"inproc":{"boundary":{"same":{"s":2,"why":"Simplest, with no network hop."},"org":{"s":-1,"why":"Another team's release cycle becomes your release cycle."},"other":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot embed code you do not own."}},"shape":{"quick":{"s":1,"why":"A function call fits a quick answer."},"long":{"s":-1,"why":"You must build your own task lifecycle."}},"internals":{"own":{"s":1,"why":"You can wrap and test it directly."},"opaque":{"s":-2,"why":"Opaque agents cannot be embedded."}}},"mcp":{"boundary":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but adds a protocol hop with no gain."},"org":{"s":1,"why":"A shared tool contract across teams."},"other":{"s":1,"why":"Works across organizations with OAuth."}},"shape":{"quick":{"s":1,"why":"A tool call fits one request and one result."},"long":{"s":-1,"why":"No task lifecycle unless both sides support the Tasks extension."}},"internals":{"own":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"opaque":{"s":1,"why":"A tool already hides its internals."}}},"a2a":{"boundary":{"same":{"s":-1,"why":"Network latency and operations with no boundary to justify them."},"org":{"s":2,"why":"Built for crossing team boundaries with discovery and tasks."},"other":{"s":2,"why":"Framework-agnostic across organizations."}},"shape":{"quick":{"s":-1,"why":"Card, auth and version handling for one quick answer."},"long":{"s":2,"why":"Tasks, input-required, streaming, push and artifacts are built in."}},"internals":{"own":{"s":-1,"why":"If you own it, embedding is simpler."},"opaque":{"s":2,"why":"Opaque execution is the design center."}}},"api":{"boundary":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"org":{"s":1,"why":"A versioned API is easy to govern."},"other":{"s":1,"why":"A stable contract works across organizations."}},"shape":{"quick":{"s":2,"why":"A deterministic call needs no agent."},"long":{"s":-2,"why":"No conversation, input-required or artifacts."}},"internals":{"own":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"opaque":{"s":1,"why":"An API hides internals too."}}}},"takeaway":"Cross a boundary only when you must. Ownership and work shape decide between a function, a tool and a peer."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes and how to detect them","items":[{"symptom":"The calling agent times out on a 20-minute job","cause":"A peer was treated like a function and called synchronously through a tool wrapper","fix":"Use tasks: return a handle, then poll, stream or use push. Detect it with a timeout rate per remote skill"},{"symptom":"Every user request pays an extra network round trip to a helper agent that lives in the same repo","cause":"A network boundary was introduced where none existed","fix":"Collapse same-team agents into in-process tools and keep A2A for real boundaries"},{"symptom":"A wrapped MCP tool loses the result when the client disconnects","cause":"No task lifecycle, so the work is tied to one connection","fix":"Use the Tasks extension on both sides, or call the agent through A2A tasks"},{"symptom":"A trace shows a gap where the remote agent ran","cause":"Opaque execution hides internals, and trace context was not propagated","fix":"Propagate W3C trace context in HTTP headers and log `taskId` and correlation ids on both sides [[r8]]"},{"symptom":"An agent call returns inconsistent totals","cause":"A deterministic lookup was given to an LLM agent","fix":"Use a plain API when the work is deterministic"}]},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"A2A v1.0.0 released","value":"2026-03-12","note":"Patch 1.0.1 on 2026-05-28 [[r4]]"},{"label":"Linux Foundation launch","value":"2025-06-23","note":"More than 100 supporting companies at launch [[r9]]"},{"label":"Protocol bindings","value":"3","note":"JSON-RPC, gRPC and HTTP+JSON [[r1]]"},{"label":"Task states","value":"9","note":"Four terminal and two interrupted [[r1]]"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"A2A does not replace MCP","md":"If asked \"A2A or MCP?\", answer with the boundary. Tools and resources for one agent use MCP. Independent agents that own their own reasoning and state use A2A. A2A's own comparison notes that well-defined, stateless skills can also be surfaced through MCP-style descriptions, but that gives up the task lifecycle [[r3]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-l01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does **opaque execution** mean in A2A?","options":["Agents encrypt every message end to end so that network monitoring tools, gateways and auditors cannot read any traffic between them at all","Agents collaborate through declared capabilities and exchanged messages without sharing internal reasoning, memory or tool implementations","Agent Cards must not describe skills","Every task is executed on the caller's infrastructure"],"answer":1,"explain":"Opacity is about internals: callers see skills, messages and artifacts, not prompts, plans or tools. It does not mean hiding traffic from monitoring, and cards still describe skills. The remote agent runs on its own infrastructure.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Select every situation where an A2A peer is a better boundary than an in-process agent-as-tool.","options":["The other agent has its own release cycle and a different owning team","A partner organization's agent was built in another framework and language","Two agents share a process and are maintained by one team","The remote work takes minutes, may ask the caller questions and returns files","The call is a deterministic lookup with a fixed schema"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Separate owners, cross-organization interoperability and a long-running, interactive, artifact-producing job all justify A2A. A shared process and team favors agent-as-tool, and a deterministic lookup is best served by a plain API.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r12"},{"id":"w09-l01-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Another team's agent runs 15-minute analyses, may ask clarifying questions and returns reports. You can reach it only over HTTPS. Which boundary fits best?","options":["A2A peer with tasks, `input-required` handling and artifacts","An in-process function wrapper that blocks until the report is ready","An MCP tool call that returns when the report is finished, with no task support","A plain API call to its web UI"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dials say another team, long-running, interactive and opaque, which is the A2A sweet spot. A blocking wrapper or a no-task MCP call ties the result to one connection and cannot ask questions. A web UI is not a contract.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w09-l01-q4","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each need to the boundary that fits best.","pairs":[{"l":"Give one agent a database query capability","r":"MCP server"},{"l":"Let a partner's agent take a task and return artifacts","r":"A2A peer"},{"l":"Call a helper agent in the same process and team","r":"Agent as a tool"},{"l":"Fetch a price with a fixed schema","r":"Plain API"}],"explain":"MCP deepens one agent with tools and data. A2A connects independent agents with tasks and artifacts. Agent-as-tool is the simplest in one process. A deterministic call needs no agent at all.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r3"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r3","r4","r8","r9","r10","r11","r12","r13","r17","r20"],"takeaways":["MCP is vertical and gives one agent tools. A2A is horizontal and connects independent, opaque agents.","Use an in-process tool when one team owns both agents. Use A2A when you cross a service, team or organization boundary.","A tool call has no task lifecycle unless the Tasks extension is supported. A2A has tasks, contexts and artifacts built in.","A2A v1.0.0 was released on 2026-03-12 and v1.0.1 on 2026-05-28."]},{"id":"w09-l02","order":2,"title":"Read the card before you trust it","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"Before you send one message you read the callee's Agent Card. The card is a claim, not proof. How you discover it, which interface you choose and how far you verify it depend on who the callee is, what the card reveals and how often it changes.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What a card claims","level":2,"id":"what-a-card-claims"},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"An Agent Card in the v1.0 shape","code":"{\n  \"name\": \"Parts Supplier Agent\",\n  \"description\": \"Checks stock, quotes prices and places orders for vehicle parts.\",\n  \"supportedInterfaces\": [\n    { \"url\": \"https://parts.example.com/a2a/v1\", \"protocolBinding\": \"JSONRPC\", \"protocolVersion\": \"1.0\" },\n    { \"url\": \"https://parts.example.com/a2a/rest\", \"protocolBinding\": \"HTTP+JSON\", \"protocolVersion\": \"1.0\", \"tenant\": \"retail\" }\n  ],\n  \"provider\": { \"organization\": \"Example Parts Ltd\", \"url\": \"https://parts.example.com\" },\n  \"version\": \"2.3.0\",\n  \"capabilities\": { \"streaming\": true, \"pushNotifications\": true, \"extendedAgentCard\": true },\n  \"securitySchemes\": {\n    \"partnerOAuth\": {\n      \"oauth2SecurityScheme\": {\n        \"flows\": {\n          \"clientCredentials\": {\n            \"tokenUrl\": \"https://login.example.com/token\",\n            \"scopes\": { \"parts.read\": \"Read stock and prices\", \"parts.order\": \"Place orders\" }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"securityRequirements\": [ { \"schemes\": { \"partnerOAuth\": { \"list\": [\"parts.read\"] } } } ],\n  \"defaultInputModes\": [\"text/plain\", \"application/json\"],\n  \"defaultOutputModes\": [\"text/plain\", \"application/json\"],\n  \"skills\": [\n    { \"id\": \"stock-check\", \"name\": \"Stock check\", \"description\": \"Reports availability and lead time for a part number.\", \"tags\": [\"parts\", \"inventory\"], \"examples\": [\"Is part 12345 in stock for a 2018 hatchback?\"] },\n    { \"id\": \"place-order\", \"name\": \"Place order\", \"description\": \"Places an order for a quoted part.\", \"tags\": [\"parts\", \"orders\"],\n      \"securityRequirements\": [ { \"schemes\": { \"partnerOAuth\": { \"list\": [\"parts.order\"] } } } ] }\n  ]\n}","caption":"Served at `/.well-known/agent-card.json`. The `securityRequirements` shape follows the sample card in section 8.5 of the spec. Required top-level fields: `name`, `description`, `supportedInterfaces`, `version`, `capabilities`, `defaultInputModes`, `defaultOutputModes`, `skills` [[r1]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Read each field as a claim and decide how far to trust it","head":["Field","What it tells you","Trust note"],"rows":[["`supportedInterfaces`","Ordered list of `url`, `protocolBinding`, `protocolVersion` and optional `tenant`. The first entry is preferred","Pick the first binding you support. Copy `tenant` into every request exactly as declared [[r1]]"],["`capabilities`","`streaming`, `pushNotifications`, `extensions` and `extendedAgentCard`","Gates operations. A missing capability returns `UnsupportedOperationError` or `PushNotificationNotSupportedError` [[r1]]"],["`skills`","`id`, `name`, `description` and `tags`, plus optional examples, modes and per-skill security","Largely descriptive. It says what the agent is likely to do well, not a contract [[r1]]"],["`securitySchemes` and `securityRequirements`","API key, HTTP, OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect or mutual TLS, and which are needed","Declares the rules. The server MUST still authenticate every request [[r1]]"],["`signatures`","Optional JWS signatures over the card","Proves integrity and origin of the card, not that the agent is safe or accurate [[r1]]"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"h","text":"Discover, then decide how far to verify","level":2,"id":"discover-then-decide-how-far-to-verify"},{"t":"compare","title":"Discovery strategies","intro":"The spec lists three ways to find a card [[r6]].","options":[{"name":"Well-known URI","tag":"GA","summary":"Fetch `https://{domain}/.well-known/agent-card.json`.","pros":["Easy to implement and standards based","Supports automated discovery"],"cons":["Open or domain-controlled by design","A public card must not carry sensitive details"],"bestWhen":"Public agents or domain-wide discovery.","avoidWhen":"The card describes internal URLs or sensitive skills.","meta":[{"k":"Setup","v":"low"},{"k":"Governance","v":"low"}]},{"name":"Curated registry","tag":"No standard API","summary":"Agents publish cards to a catalog that clients query by skill, tag or provider.","pros":["Central management and capability-based search","Can return different cards by caller identity"],"cons":["You deploy and run it","The spec does not prescribe a registry API yet"],"bestWhen":"Enterprise estates or marketplaces.","avoidWhen":"A handful of known partners.","meta":[{"k":"Setup","v":"high"},{"k":"Governance","v":"high"}]},{"name":"Direct configuration","tag":"Pinned","summary":"The client is configured with card URLs or card content.","pros":["Straightforward for known, static relationships","Nothing published to the internet"],"cons":["Inflexible for dynamic discovery","Card changes need client reconfiguration"],"bestWhen":"Tightly coupled systems and a few partners.","avoidWhen":"Many agents that change often.","meta":[{"k":"Setup","v":"low"},{"k":"Governance","v":"manual"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w09-l02-trust","title":"Choose a discovery and verification approach","prompt":"Flip where callees live, whether cards hold sensitive details and how often cards change.","dials":[{"id":"scope","label":"Where callees live","values":[{"id":"partner","label":"A few known partners"},{"id":"company","label":"Many agents inside the company"},{"id":"open","label":"Unknown parties on the internet"}]},{"id":"sensitivity","label":"Cards reveal sensitive skills or internal URLs","values":[{"id":"no","label":"No"},{"id":"yes","label":"Yes"}]},{"id":"change","label":"How often cards change","values":[{"id":"rare","label":"Rarely"},{"id":"often","label":"Often"}]}],"options":[{"id":"wk","name":"Well-known URI","summary":"Public card at a standard path."},{"id":"reg","name":"Curated registry","summary":"Catalog with selective disclosure."},{"id":"pin","name":"Pinned configuration","summary":"Card URLs or content fixed in config."},{"id":"sig","name":"Registry plus signed cards","summary":"Registry discovery and JWS verification on every card."}],"scores":{"wk":{"scope":{"partner":{"s":1,"why":"Easy for a partner to publish and for you to fetch."},"company":{"s":0,"why":"No central search or governance."},"open":{"s":2,"why":"Built for broad discovery on a domain."}},"sensitivity":{"no":{"s":1,"why":"A public card is fine when nothing in it is sensitive."},"yes":{"s":-2,"why":"Never put sensitive details in a public card. Use an authenticated extended card."}},"change":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"often":{"s":1,"why":"Clients refetch with conditional requests, so changes reach callers."}}},"reg":{"scope":{"partner":{"s":-1,"why":"Heavy for a few partners."},"company":{"s":2,"why":"Central governance and search by skill."},"open":{"s":1,"why":"Works for marketplaces, but you decide whom to trust."}},"sensitivity":{"no":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"yes":{"s":1,"why":"Registries can return different cards to different callers."}},"change":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"often":{"s":1,"why":"Cards are republished in one place."}}},"pin":{"scope":{"partner":{"s":2,"why":"A stable, known relationship."},"company":{"s":-1,"why":"Does not scale to many agents."},"open":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot pin what you have not met."}},"sensitivity":{"no":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"yes":{"s":1,"why":"Nothing is published for strangers to read."}},"change":{"rare":{"s":1,"why":"Few reconfigurations."},"often":{"s":-2,"why":"Every card change needs a client reconfiguration."}}},"sig":{"scope":{"partner":{"s":0,"why":"You already know whom to trust."},"company":{"s":1,"why":"Integrity checks help when many teams publish."},"open":{"s":2,"why":"Origin and integrity matter most for cards from unknown parties."}},"sensitivity":{"no":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"yes":{"s":0,"why":"A signature protects integrity, not confidentiality."}},"change":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"often":{"s":-1,"why":"Every change needs re-signing and key management."}}}},"takeaway":"Public and sensitive do not mix. Pick the discovery path by where callees live, then add signatures where origin matters."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes and how to detect them","items":[{"symptom":"Calls fail with `UnsupportedOperationError` on streaming","cause":"The client ignored `capabilities.streaming` and called `SendStreamingMessage`","fix":"Read capabilities first and fall back to polling. Log capability mismatches"},{"symptom":"Traffic flows to an unexpected endpoint after a card refresh","cause":"A card was trusted without TLS validation, signature checks or pinning","fix":"Validate the server certificate, verify signatures where present and alert when interface URLs change"},{"symptom":"Internal URLs and sensitive skills appear in a public card","cause":"One card served to everyone","fix":"Move detail into an authenticated extended card or a registry that discloses by caller, and put access controls on the card endpoint"},{"symptom":"Clients use stale cards after an upgrade","cause":"No `Cache-Control: max-age` and `ETag`, or a fixed cache duration","fix":"Servers send both headers. Clients use conditional requests with `If-None-Match`"},{"symptom":"Requests are rejected on a multi-agent endpoint","cause":"The client omitted or altered `tenant` from the chosen interface","fix":"Copy the interface's `tenant` exactly into every request, and omit it when the interface has none"}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Card signing in four steps","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["Apply the spec's field-presence rules so the JSON matches what was set, then canonicalize with the JSON Canonicalization Scheme (RFC 8785), excluding the `signatures` field [[r1]] [[r19]].","Sign the canonical form as a JSON Web Signature (RFC 7515). Each signature has a base64url `protected` header and a base64url `signature`, and may carry an unprotected `header` [[r1]] [[r18]].","Publish the card with its `signatures` array.","Verify against a key you trust before using the card. Signing is optional (the spec says Agent Cards MAY be signed) [[r1]]."]}]},{"title":"Extended Agent Card and tenant","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["`capabilities.extendedAgentCard: true` means authenticated clients can call `GetExtendedAgentCard` for extra skills or detail. The call MUST require authentication, and clients SHOULD replace their cached public card for the session [[r1]].","If the capability is absent or false the call returns `UnsupportedOperationError`. If it is true but no extended card is configured the agent returns `ExtendedAgentCardNotConfiguredError` [[r1]].","Extended cards SHOULD NOT contain secrets such as unmasked credentials or internal URLs that would be dangerous if leaked [[r1]].","`tenant` lets one endpoint host many agents. When an interface declares it, clients MUST include it in every request [[r1]]."]}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-l02-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A client log and the callee's card are shown. Which cause fits best?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"card:   \"capabilities\": { \"streaming\": false }\nclient: SendStreamingMessage -> UnsupportedOperationError: Streaming is not supported by the agent"},"options":["The callee's TLS certificate expired","The client used the REST binding and the card declared JSON-RPC","The client skipped the card's `capabilities` and called an operation the agent does not offer","The task is in a terminal state and cannot accept messages"],"answer":2,"explain":"If `capabilities.streaming` is false or absent, streaming operations MUST return `UnsupportedOperationError`. A TLS failure would not reach an operation-level error. Binding mismatch would fail differently, and a terminal task produces a different message for a different reason.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l02-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every true statement about securing Agent Cards.","options":["Sensitive detail belongs behind an authenticated extended card","A card MAY be signed as a JWS over its RFC 8785 canonical form","A valid signature proves the agent's skills are accurate and safe","`securitySchemes` in the card enforce authentication without any server checks","Servers SHOULD send `Cache-Control: max-age` and an `ETag` for the card"],"answers":[0,1,4],"explain":"Extended cards, optional JWS signing and HTTP caching headers are in the spec. A signature proves integrity and origin, not accuracy or safety. The card only declares schemes, and the server MUST authenticate every request.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l02-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"You must discover hundreds of internal agents. Some skills are sensitive, and authors update cards every week. Which approach fits best?","options":["A curated registry that discloses cards by caller identity","Pin each card URL in client configuration, reviewed at each release","Rely on a public well-known URI for every agent, with sensitive skills described in plain text","Ask each team to email the card to every client team"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hundreds of agents with frequent change and sensitive skills point to a registry with selective disclosure. Pinning needs reconfiguration at every change, and public cards expose sensitive skills. Email is not discovery. The spec does not yet define a registry API, so you run it yourself.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r6","r18","r19"],"takeaways":["A card is a discovery claim. It declares interfaces, skills, capabilities and security, and the server still authenticates every request.","Pick the first supported interface, send `tenant` exactly as declared and check capabilities before calling an operation.","Skills are descriptive, not a contract. Signatures prove origin and integrity only.","Public cards must not carry sensitive detail. Use an authenticated extended card or selective disclosure.","The spec does not define a registry API, so you design that layer yourself."]},{"id":"w09-l03","order":3,"title":"Messages, tasks and artifacts","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A remote agent gets a request that may be a quick answer, a long job or a conversation. How it models the reply, the state and the follow-ups decides whether callers can reason about the work. Choose a response style and learn the rules that keep ids and states consistent.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The objects and the lifecycle","level":2,"id":"the-objects-and-the-lifecycle"},{"t":"table","caption":"Core objects in the v1.0 spec [[r1]]","head":["Object","Key fields","Rules worth knowing"],"rows":[["Task","`id`, `contextId`, `status`, `artifacts`, `history`","The server generates `id`. It is the stateful unit of work with a lifecycle"],["Message","`messageId`, `role`, `parts`, optional `taskId`, `contextId`, `referenceTaskIds`","Roles are `ROLE_USER` and `ROLE_AGENT`. If both ids are given they must match, and with only `taskId` the server infers the context"],["Part","Exactly one of `text`, `raw`, `url` or `data`, plus `mediaType`, `filename`, `metadata`","`raw` is base64 in JSON. The v0.3 `kind` discriminator is gone"],["Artifact","`artifactId`, `parts`, optional `name`, `description`","A task output. `artifactId` is unique within the task and at least one part is required"]]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-l03-d1","title":"Typical task lifecycle","alt":"A task is submitted and then working. It can pause in input required or auth required and return to working. It ends in completed, failed, canceled or rejected.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> SUBMITTED\n  SUBMITTED --> WORKING\n  SUBMITTED --> REJECTED\n  WORKING --> INPUT_REQUIRED\n  INPUT_REQUIRED --> WORKING: client replies\n  WORKING --> AUTH_REQUIRED\n  AUTH_REQUIRED --> WORKING: credential obtained\n  WORKING --> COMPLETED\n  WORKING --> FAILED\n  WORKING --> CANCELED\n  COMPLETED --> [*]","caption":"State names carry the prefix `TASK_STATE_` on the wire. Terminal: COMPLETED, FAILED, CANCELED, REJECTED. Interrupted: INPUT_REQUIRED and AUTH_REQUIRED. The spec defines the states and says terminal tasks accept no more messages. It does not publish a strict transition table, so treat the arrows as the usual flow [[r1]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"How should an agent respond to a message?","intro":"Three styles from the Life of a Task guide [[r5]].","options":[{"name":"Message-only agent","tag":"Stateless","summary":"Always answer with a Message and use `contextId` to tie turns together.","pros":["Simplest to build","No task state to store"],"cons":["No progress tracking or interrupted states","Poor fit for long work"],"bestWhen":"The agent wraps a quick LLM call or simple tools.","avoidWhen":"Work is long-running or needs input-required handling.","meta":[{"k":"State","v":"context only"},{"k":"Overhead","v":"low"}]},{"name":"Task-generating agent","tag":"Uniform","summary":"Always return a Task, even for a plain answer, which becomes a completed task.","pros":["One model for every request","Every unit of work is trackable and attributable"],"cons":["Completed task objects for trivial exchanges","Once a task exists, later replies are tasks too"],"bestWhen":"You need an audit trail and retries per request.","avoidWhen":"Chatty, lightweight interactions dominate.","meta":[{"k":"State","v":"per task"},{"k":"Overhead","v":"medium"}]},{"name":"Hybrid agent","tag":"Negotiate, then commit","summary":"Use Messages to agree on scope, then create a Task for committed work.","pros":["Cheap clarification before costly work","Task tracking only where it pays"],"cons":["More branching logic","Callers must handle both result shapes"],"bestWhen":"Scope needs a short dialogue before a long job.","avoidWhen":"A tiny agent with one fixed behavior.","meta":[{"k":"State","v":"context, then task"},{"k":"Overhead","v":"medium"}]}]},{"t":"flow","title":"A multi-turn exchange, end to end","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Send","md":"Client calls `SendMessage` with a message and no ids.","tag":"client"},{"title":"Task","md":"Agent returns a Task with a server-made `taskId` and `contextId`.","tag":"agent"},{"title":"Ask","md":"State becomes `INPUT_REQUIRED` with a status message.","tag":"agent"},{"title":"Reply","md":"Client sends the same `taskId` and `contextId` with its answer.","tag":"client"},{"title":"Done","md":"State `COMPLETED` with an Artifact.","tag":"agent"},{"title":"Refine","md":"A new message with the same `contextId` and `referenceTaskIds` starts a new task.","tag":"client"}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes and how to detect them","items":[{"symptom":"A follow-up returns `UnsupportedOperationError`","cause":"The client sent a message to a task in a terminal state. Terminal tasks cannot restart","fix":"Start a new task in the same `contextId` and set `referenceTaskIds`. Detect it by counting errors on terminal tasks"},{"symptom":"An answer to a clarification starts a fresh task","cause":"The reply carried only `contextId`. A `contextId` without `taskId` starts a new task in that conversation","fix":"Include the original `taskId` as well as `contextId` when resuming"},{"symptom":"Requests with client-made ids are rejected","cause":"Clients cannot create tasks with their own `taskId`, and a `contextId` that does not match the task is rejected","fix":"Let the server generate ids, treat `contextId` as opaque and echo ids unchanged"},{"symptom":"Users see tasks that belong to someone else","cause":"`GetTask` or `ListTasks` was not scoped to the authenticated caller","fix":"Check authorization before any query. Return `TaskNotFoundError` for tasks the caller cannot access [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"A caller shows an outdated report after a refinement","cause":"Refinements create new artifacts and the protocol does not track versions","fix":"Clients keep a version history. Agents reuse a consistent artifact name for refined versions [[r5]]"}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Operations and where they live on the wire","blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Method mapping across bindings [[r1]]","head":["Operation","JSON-RPC and gRPC method","HTTP+JSON endpoint"],"rows":[["Send a message","`SendMessage`","`POST /message:send`"],["Send and stream","`SendStreamingMessage`","`POST /message:stream`"],["Get a task","`GetTask`","`GET /tasks/{id}`"],["List tasks","`ListTasks`","`GET /tasks`"],["Cancel a task","`CancelTask`","`POST /tasks/{id}:cancel`"],["Subscribe to a task","`SubscribeToTask`","`POST /tasks/{id}:subscribe`"],["Push configuration (create, get, list, delete)","Four `...TaskPushNotificationConfig` operations","`/tasks/{id}/pushNotificationConfigs`"],["Get the extended card","`GetExtendedAgentCard`","`GET /extendedAgentCard`"]]},{"t":"p","md":"`ListTasks` uses cursor pagination and is scoped to the caller. Cancellation is idempotent. A duplicate cancel MAY return `TaskNotFoundError` if the task was already canceled and purged [[r1]]."}]},{"title":"Named errors","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["`TaskNotFoundError`: the id does not exist or is not accessible","`TaskNotCancelableError`: the task is not in a cancelable state","`UnsupportedOperationError`: terminal task, or streaming or the extended card is not supported","`PushNotificationNotSupportedError`: push is not declared in the card","`ContentTypeNotSupportedError`: a part's media type is not accepted","`ExtensionSupportRequiredError` and `VersionNotSupportedError`: a required extension or the requested version is missing"]}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-l03-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put a typical multi-turn A2A exchange in order.","items":["Client sends `SendMessage` with a message and no ids","Agent returns a Task with server-generated `taskId` and `contextId`","Task moves to `INPUT_REQUIRED` and carries a question","Client replies with the same `taskId` and `contextId`","Task reaches `COMPLETED` with an Artifact"],"explain":"Clients cannot supply a new `taskId`, so the server mints ids on the first response. The question pauses the task in an interrupted state. The resume must reuse both ids. Completion produces the artifact.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l03-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"The client log is shown. What is the correct fix?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"t=0   SendMessage -> Task id=t-77 contextId=c-1 state=TASK_STATE_COMPLETED\nt=90  SendMessage taskId=t-77 contextId=c-1 \"add a summary\" -> UnsupportedOperationError"},"options":["Retry the same call after a short delay, because the agent was briefly unavailable and a terminal state clears itself after some minutes of inactivity","Send the follow-up as a new message in `contextId` c-1 and reference `t-77` in `referenceTaskIds`, so the agent creates a new task","Remove `contextId` so the server accepts the old `taskId`","Generate a fresh `taskId` on the client for the follow-up"],"answer":1,"explain":"`COMPLETED` is terminal and terminal tasks cannot accept messages or restart. A refinement is a new task in the same context. Retrying cannot help, removing `contextId` does not reopen the task, and clients cannot create their own task ids.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"},{"id":"w09-l03-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each A2A object or state to its role.","pairs":[{"l":"Task","r":"The stateful unit of work with a lifecycle"},{"l":"Part","r":"Exactly one of text, raw bytes, a URL or structured data"},{"l":"`TASK_STATE_INPUT_REQUIRED`","r":"An interrupted state that waits for the client"},{"l":"`TASK_STATE_REJECTED`","r":"A terminal state where the agent decided not to perform the task"}],"explain":"A Task carries status, artifacts and history. A Part is a union of four content forms. Input-required is interrupted, not terminal. Rejected is terminal and means the agent declined the work.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r5"],"takeaways":["The server mints `taskId` and `contextId`. Clients echo them. A `contextId` without a `taskId` starts a new task.","Terminal tasks are immutable. Refinements start new tasks in the same context and reference earlier ones.","Choose message-only, task-generating or hybrid by whether work is long-running and needs tracking.","Scope every task operation to the authenticated caller and return `TaskNotFoundError` for anything else."]},{"id":"w09-l04","order":4,"title":"Wait, stream or push","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A remote agent's job takes anywhere from two seconds to two days, and your caller may sit behind a firewall, on a phone or in a serverless function. Duration, reachability and update frequency are open. Choose how updates arrive, and learn how bindings and versions affect that choice.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three ways to hear back","level":2,"id":"three-ways-to-hear-back"},{"t":"compare","title":"Update delivery in A2A","intro":"The spec offers three complementary mechanisms [[r1]] [[r7]].","options":[{"name":"Polling","tag":"Always available","summary":"Call `GetTask` periodically.","pros":["Simple and works with every binding","Works behind restrictive firewalls"],"cons":["Higher latency and wasted requests","Load grows with callers times polling rate"],"bestWhen":"Infrequent updates, simple clients or no inbound connectivity.","avoidWhen":"Many callers need frequent progress.","meta":[{"k":"Needs","v":"nothing declared"},{"k":"Connection","v":"short"}]},{"name":"Streaming (SSE or native)","tag":"Needs streaming capability","summary":"`SendStreamingMessage` or `SubscribeToTask` delivers events in order as they happen.","pros":["Low latency and efficient for frequent updates","Reconnect to a live task with `SubscribeToTask`, whose first event is the current Task"],"cons":["Needs a persistent connection","Proxies can buffer or cut streams"],"bestWhen":"Interactive apps, dashboards and live progress.","avoidWhen":"Tasks run for hours or the client cannot hold a connection.","meta":[{"k":"Needs","v":"`capabilities.streaming`"},{"k":"Connection","v":"persistent"}]},{"name":"Push notifications","tag":"Needs push capability","summary":"The agent POSTs updates to a webhook the client registered.","pros":["No persistent connection","Suits minutes, hours or days"],"cons":["The client must be reachable over HTTPS","Adds SSRF, replay and forgery defenses"],"bestWhen":"Server-to-server integrations and very long tasks.","avoidWhen":"Mobile or firewalled clients that cannot receive inbound calls.","meta":[{"k":"Needs","v":"`capabilities.pushNotifications`"},{"k":"Connection","v":"none held"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w09-l04-delivery","title":"Pick a delivery mode","prompt":"Flip task duration, whether the callee can reach you and how much progress you need.","dials":[{"id":"duration","label":"Task duration","values":[{"id":"seconds","label":"Seconds"},{"id":"minutes","label":"Minutes"},{"id":"days","label":"Hours or days"}]},{"id":"reach","label":"Callee can POST to your webhook","values":[{"id":"no","label":"No (firewall, mobile)"},{"id":"yes","label":"Yes"}]},{"id":"updates","label":"Updates you need","values":[{"id":"final","label":"Final result only"},{"id":"progress","label":"Frequent progress"}]}],"options":[{"id":"block","name":"Blocking SendMessage","summary":"The default: wait for a terminal or interrupted state."},{"id":"poll","name":"Poll with GetTask","summary":"Return immediately, then ask."},{"id":"stream","name":"Stream","summary":"Hold a connection and receive events."},{"id":"push","name":"Push webhook","summary":"Let the agent call you."}],"scores":{"block":{"duration":{"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"A short call returns before any timeout."},"minutes":{"s":-1,"why":"Connections and proxies time out."},"days":{"s":-2,"why":"No connection survives that long."}},"reach":{"no":{"s":0,"why":"Needs only an outbound call."},"yes":{"s":0,"why":"Not used."}},"updates":{"final":{"s":1,"why":"One answer is all you need."},"progress":{"s":-2,"why":"No progress until the end."}}},"poll":{"duration":{"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"Extra round trips for a quick answer."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"A modest polling rate is cheap."},"days":{"s":1,"why":"Works, with a long interval."}},"reach":{"no":{"s":2,"why":"Works behind firewalls and on every binding."},"yes":{"s":0,"why":"Works, though push may be cheaper."}},"updates":{"final":{"s":1,"why":"Fine when you need only the result."},"progress":{"s":-1,"why":"Latency and wasted polls."}}},"stream":{"duration":{"seconds":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"minutes":{"s":2,"why":"Ideal for live progress while a connection is practical."},"days":{"s":-1,"why":"Holding a connection for hours is fragile. Reconnect with `SubscribeToTask`."}},"reach":{"no":{"s":1,"why":"Only the client opens a connection."},"yes":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"updates":{"final":{"s":0,"why":"More than you need."},"progress":{"s":2,"why":"Events arrive in order as they happen."}}},"push":{"duration":{"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"Webhook setup for a quick call."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but a stream is often simpler."},"days":{"s":2,"why":"No connection to hold across hours or days."}},"reach":{"no":{"s":-2,"why":"Cannot receive webhooks."},"yes":{"s":2,"why":"Designed for reachable server-to-server clients."}},"updates":{"final":{"s":1,"why":"One notification on completion."},"progress":{"s":0,"why":"Possible, but each event is a POST."}}}},"takeaway":"Duration and reachability decide between a held connection and a webhook. Polling is the baseline that always works."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-l04-d1","title":"Push notification flow","alt":"The client sends a message with a push configuration and asks to return immediately. The agent returns a submitted task and later posts status and completion events to the client's webhook, which answers 2xx.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant A as Agent\n  participant W as Webhook\n  C->>A: SendMessage with taskPushNotificationConfig\n  A-->>C: Task in SUBMITTED state\n  A->>W: POST statusUpdate with credentials\n  W-->>A: 2xx\n  A->>W: POST task with artifacts on completion\n  W-->>A: 2xx","caption":"The push configuration can ride in `SendMessageConfiguration.taskPushNotificationConfig`, with an empty task id, or be created later with the push configuration operations. Payloads use the same `StreamResponse` format as streaming [[r1]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"SendMessage waits by default","md":"`SendMessageConfiguration.returnImmediately` defaults to false. The call then waits until the task reaches a terminal or interrupted state. Set it to true to get the task back at once and then poll, stream or rely on push. The flag has no effect when the agent answers with a direct Message, or for streaming calls. The operation summary in section 3.1.1 phrases the return more loosely than the configuration section, so test your SDK's behavior [[r1]]."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes and how to detect them","items":[{"symptom":"The caller hangs for the whole job on `SendMessage`","cause":"The default blocking mode was used for a long task","fix":"Set `returnImmediately` to true and poll, stream or use push. Alert on call durations above your timeout budget"},{"symptom":"A dropped stream loses the result","cause":"The client treated the stream as the task. Task lifecycle is independent of any stream","fix":"Reconnect with `SubscribeToTask`, which returns the current Task first so no update is missed, or fall back to `GetTask`"},{"symptom":"The agent's webhook calls reach internal services","cause":"Client-supplied webhook URLs were trusted. That is an SSRF and DDoS amplification risk","fix":"Reject private, loopback and link-local ranges, use allowlists or ownership challenges, and apply egress controls [[r7]]"},{"symptom":"Duplicate or forged notifications change state","cause":"The receiver did not verify credentials, task id or freshness","fix":"Verify credentials, check the task id, reply 2xx, process idempotently and reject stale timestamps or reused ids"},{"symptom":"A 0.3 client fails to parse replies from a 1.0 agent","cause":"Enum casing, the `kind` discriminator and operation names changed, and the version was not negotiated","fix":"Send `A2A-Version` on every request and keep a dual-support phase while clients migrate"}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Three bindings, one behavior","blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"All supported bindings MUST be functionally equivalent [[r1]]","head":["Binding","Wire format","Streaming","Notes"],"rows":[["JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP","`application/json`, PascalCase method names","SSE","`SendMessage`, `GetTask` and so on"],["gRPC","Protocol buffers at `host:port`","Native streams","Same operations and error mapping"],["HTTP+JSON","REST paths such as `POST /message:send`. `application/a2a+json` SHOULD be used","SSE","Patch 1.0.1 clarified the media type"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Service parameters `A2A-Version` and `A2A-Extensions` travel as HTTP headers. Clients choose any binding the card declares and SHOULD fall back to another when one fails. A `tenant` field lets one endpoint host many agents [[r1]]."}]},{"title":"Versions and migrating from 0.3 to 1.0","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Clients send `A2A-Version: Major.Minor`. An empty header means 0.3, and an unsupported version returns `VersionNotSupportedError`. Patch numbers are never negotiated [[r1]].","Enum values became SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE with prefixes such as `TASK_STATE_` and `ROLE_`. The `kind` discriminator and the `final` flag on status events were removed [[r2]].","Operations were renamed, for example `message/send` to `SendMessage`. `protocolVersion` moved into each interface. `supportsAuthenticatedExtendedCard` became `capabilities.extendedAgentCard`. `ListTasks`, `tenant` and cursor pagination are new [[r2]].","The migration guide suggests three phases: a compatibility layer, dual support, then v1.0 only [[r2]].","The migration page says the implicit and password OAuth flows were removed, while the normative spec still lists them as Deprecated. The spec is authoritative [[r1]] [[r2]]."]}]},{"title":"Verifying webhooks with JWT and JWKS","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["The client registers `authentication.scheme` of `Bearer` and an optional expected issuer or audience.","The agent signs a JWT with `iss`, `aud`, `iat`, `exp`, `jti` and `taskId`, and publishes its public keys at a JWKS endpoint.","The webhook reads the `kid`, fetches the key (cache it), verifies the signature and validates the claims.","The webhook also checks the `token` from the push configuration, rejects stale timestamps and refuses reused `jti` values [[r7]]."]}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-l04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"1,000 callers each poll `GetTask` every 5 seconds while a 3-hour task runs. How many `GetTask` requests per hour does the agent absorb?","answer":720000,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"requests per hour","hint":"callers x (3600 / interval)","explain":"Each caller makes 3,600 divided by 5, or 720, requests per hour. Times 1,000 callers is 720,000 per hour. Push or streaming replaces this constant load with events only when state changes, which is why long tasks favor push.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l04-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A caller's `SendMessage` blocks for 40 minutes. The card declares `pushNotifications: true` and the request set no configuration. What explains it?","options":["Push notifications must first be activated by a separate administrator call before any message can return a task handle","`returnImmediately` defaults to false, so the call waits for a terminal or interrupted state","The agent is using the gRPC binding, which is always synchronous","A2A forbids tasks longer than 30 minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"With the default blocking mode the operation waits until the task is terminal or interrupted. Setting `returnImmediately: true` returns the task at once. Push configuration does not change blocking, gRPC is not synchronous by nature, and the spec sets no task duration limit.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every defense that fits A2A push notifications.","options":["The agent rejects webhook URLs that point to private, loopback or link-local addresses","The receiver verifies the credentials and that the task id is one it created","The receiver processes notifications idempotently because duplicates can occur","The agent follows redirects to internal hosts so delivery always succeeds","The agent removes timeouts so slow webhooks never fail"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"URL validation prevents SSRF, receiver verification prevents forgery, and idempotent handling tolerates retries. Following redirects to internal hosts defeats SSRF controls, and the spec recommends 10 to 30 second timeouts with exponential backoff.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"},{"id":"w09-l04-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"The callee runs 20-minute jobs. Your client sits behind a corporate firewall that blocks inbound calls and needs only the final result. Which delivery mode fits best?","options":["Push notifications to a public webhook","A held SSE stream for the full 20 minutes","Blocking `SendMessage` with the default settings","`SendMessage` with `returnImmediately: true`, then poll `GetTask` at a modest interval"],"answer":3,"explain":"A firewall blocks webhooks and a 20-minute stream or blocking call is fragile. Returning immediately and polling needs only outbound connectivity, and a modest interval keeps load low when only the final result matters.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r7"],"takeaways":["Polling always works, streaming suits live progress, push suits long tasks and reachable callers.","`SendMessage` waits by default. Set `returnImmediately: true` for a handle.","Webhooks need SSRF, authenticity and replay defenses. Reconnect streams with `SubscribeToTask`.","Send `A2A-Version` on every request and plan dual support when clients move from 0.3 to 1.0."]},{"id":"w09-l05","order":5,"title":"Trust between strangers, and the Microsoft stack","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Your agent will call agents you do not own, and others will call yours. Identity, delegation, consent and the platform you run on shape what is possible. Decide how callers authenticate, what a credential request inside a task may do, and what Foundry and Agent Framework support.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Authentication happens on every request","level":2,"id":"authentication-happens-on-every-request"},{"t":"table","caption":"How A2A layers trust. A2A treats agents as standard enterprise applications and leaves identity to HTTP standards, where MCP defines its own OAuth profile [[r1]] [[r8]] [[r21]]","head":["Layer","Rule","Consequence for design"],"rows":[["Transport","Production deployments MUST use HTTPS or TLS. Clients SHOULD verify the server certificate","No plain HTTP between agents, including in the same network"],["Identity","No user or client identity travels in the payload. Credentials are obtained out of band and sent on every request in HTTP headers or metadata","Delegation must be built from tokens, not message fields"],["Authentication","A server MUST authenticate every incoming request against the schemes declared in its card","A card that lists a scheme does not enforce it by itself"],["Authorization","Implementation-specific. It may consider skills, actions within tasks, data policies and OAuth scopes. Task listing, get, cancel and subscribe MUST be scoped to the caller","Check authorization before any database query that could reveal other callers' tasks"],["In-task authorization","An agent can move a task to `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` and ask the client to fulfill a credential request, ideally out of band","The state alone authorizes nothing. Scope and lifetime are yours to define"],["Observability","Participate in distributed tracing with W3C Trace Context headers, log `taskId` and correlation ids, audit sensitive events","Fronting A2A servers with API management is highly recommended across organizations"]]},{"t":"dials","id":"w09-l05-scheme","title":"Pick a security scheme for the card","prompt":"Flip where the caller sits, whose authority the call carries and how many callers you serve.","dials":[{"id":"boundary","label":"Where the caller sits","values":[{"id":"tenant","label":"Same tenant or company"},{"id":"partner","label":"A partner organization"}]},{"id":"identity","label":"Whose authority the call carries","values":[{"id":"agent","label":"The calling agent's own"},{"id":"user","label":"The end user's"}]},{"id":"scale","label":"Number of callers","values":[{"id":"few","label":"A few"},{"id":"many","label":"Many"}]}],"options":[{"id":"key","name":"API key","summary":"A shared secret in a header, query or cookie."},{"id":"cc","name":"OAuth 2.0 client credentials","summary":"Machine identity with scopes."},{"id":"code","name":"OAuth 2.0 authorization code","summary":"User-delegated access with PKCE."},{"id":"oidc","name":"OpenID Connect","summary":"Discovery through the identity provider's metadata."},{"id":"mtls","name":"Mutual TLS","summary":"Client certificates on the channel."}],"scores":{"key":{"boundary":{"tenant":{"s":1,"why":"Simple inside one trust domain."},"partner":{"s":-1,"why":"A shared secret across organizations is hard to scope and rotate."}},"identity":{"agent":{"s":0,"why":"Identifies the app only."},"user":{"s":-2,"why":"Carries no user identity."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":1,"why":"Few keys to manage."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Key distribution and rotation do not scale."}}},"cc":{"boundary":{"tenant":{"s":1,"why":"Machine identities with scopes fit internal agents."},"partner":{"s":2,"why":"Per-partner clients with narrow scopes."}},"identity":{"agent":{"s":2,"why":"The agent acts as itself."},"user":{"s":-2,"why":"The user is lost."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Standard token issuance scales and revokes centrally."}}},"code":{"boundary":{"tenant":{"s":0,"why":"Works, with consent flows."},"partner":{"s":1,"why":"Users sign in through their own provider."}},"identity":{"agent":{"s":-2,"why":"No user to consent."},"user":{"s":2,"why":"The token carries the user's authority."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"oidc":{"boundary":{"tenant":{"s":2,"why":"Reuse the enterprise identity provider and its discovery URL."},"partner":{"s":0,"why":"Needs a federation arrangement."}},"identity":{"agent":{"s":1,"why":"Service identities work."},"user":{"s":1,"why":"User tokens work."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Central provider management."}}},"mtls":{"boundary":{"tenant":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"partner":{"s":1,"why":"Strong channel binding between known organizations."}},"identity":{"agent":{"s":1,"why":"Certificates identify the calling service."},"user":{"s":-2,"why":"A certificate says nothing about a user."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":1,"why":"Few certificates to manage."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"Certificate management grows with callers."}}}},"takeaway":"A user in the loop points to authorization code. A service identity points to client credentials or mutual TLS. Keys are the cheapest and the hardest to scope."},{"t":"table","caption":"A2A on Microsoft platforms as of 2026-10-13","head":["Capability","Foundry Agent Service","Microsoft Agent Framework"],"rows":[["Call a remote A2A agent","Tool type `a2a` targets A2A 1.0 and is generally available. `a2a_preview` targets 0.3 and is in preview. A project connection stores auth: `none`, `custom-keys`, `oauth2`, `user-entra-token`, `project-managed-identity` or `agentic-identity` [[r16]]","`A2AAgent` wraps a remote endpoint as an agent. .NET resolves the card with `A2ACardResolver`. Packages are prerelease [[r15]]"],["Expose your agent over A2A","Incoming A2A for v1.0: JSON-RPC only, text only, no streaming, requires the responses protocol, Entra auth only [[r17]]",".NET `MapA2AHttpJson`. Python `A2AExecutor`. Go has a provider package. Prerelease [[r14]]"],["Versions","Serves 1.0 and preview 0.3. With no `A2A-Version` header or `a2a-version` query value it serves 0.3. Header and query must agree or Foundry returns 400 [[r17]]","Not stated on the pages reviewed. Check the SDK release notes before relying on a version"],["Conversation state","Tasks and contexts are kept 60 days from the most recent write [[r17]]","The A2A `context_id` becomes the session id. If the remote restarts and loses state, context may be lost [[r14]] [[r12]]"],["Legacy","The Connected Agents tool is not available in the new Foundry Agent Service. Use the A2A tool [[r16]]","Agents as tools remain the in-process option [[r13]]"]]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes and how to detect them","items":[{"symptom":"401 or 403 when calling a Foundry agent over A2A with a valid Entra token","cause":"The calling identity lacks the Foundry Agent Consumer role on the project or agent. The role goes to the principal's object id","fix":"Assign the role at the narrowest scope that works. For OBO grant the user or a group, for service calls grant the agent identity, service principal or managed identity"},{"symptom":"Replies arrive in the 0.3 shape","cause":"No version selector was sent, so Foundry served preview 0.3","fix":"Send `A2A-Version: 1.0`, use the `agentCard/v1.0` URL, or the query value. Keep header and query consistent"},{"symptom":"A destructive step ran after the client replied to `AUTH_REQUIRED`","cause":"The agent treated the state change or the credential as authorization for the operation","fix":"The spec says the agent MUST NOT treat the state transition as authorization. Check the specific operation and scope after the credential arrives"},{"symptom":"A credential travelled through three agents and was reused","cause":"In-band credential exchange across a chain of agents exposes it to each hop","fix":"Prefer out-of-band delivery. If in-band, bind the credential to the originating agent and encrypt it for that agent"},{"symptom":"Conversation context vanished after two months or a restart","cause":"Foundry's 60-day retention from the last write, or a remote agent that restarted without durable state","fix":"Persist your own conversation summary and design for expiry. Reset the clock only by writing to the task"}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"In-task authorization with AUTH_REQUIRED","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["The agent MUST track the work as a Task, move it to `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` and include a status message that explains the need, unless negotiated out of band.","Credentials SHOULD arrive out of band over a secure channel. If received that way, the agent SHOULD keep active streams open and MAY continue without a follow-up message.","A client that is itself an A2A agent can pass the request up by moving its own task to `AUTH_REQUIRED`, forming a chain.","Clients that lack a live stream risk missing the resume, so they should subscribe, register a webhook or poll.","Credentials obtained for one request MUST NOT be assumed to authorize later messages on the task unless the implementation, issuer or an extension says so [[r1]]."]}]},{"title":"Foundry identity patterns for incoming A2A","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Incoming A2A requires Microsoft Entra ID authentication. Key-based and anonymous access, even to the agent card, are not supported [[r17]].","On-behalf-of: the caller passes the end user's identity, so the agent can scope actions to that user's permissions.","Service identity: the caller authenticates as an agent identity, service principal or managed identity, which suits backend workflows where no user context is needed [[r17]].","To call a Foundry agent target with the A2A tool, use the target's A2A base path, `agentic-identity` auth and the audience `https://ai.azure.com`. To read a card that requires authentication, set `send_credentials_for_agent_card` to true [[r16]]."]}]},{"title":"Agent Framework patterns","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":[".NET hosting uses `Microsoft.Agents.AI.Hosting.A2A.AspNetCore` with `MapA2AHttpJson`, installed with `--prerelease`. The client package is `Microsoft.Agents.AI.A2A` [[r14]] [[r15]].","Python uses `pip install agent-framework-a2a --pre` for `A2AAgent` (client) and `A2AExecutor` (server, built on `a2a-sdk`) [[r14]] [[r15]].","`A2AAgent` is a transport-level wrapper. Whatever tools the remote agent uses stay on the remote side and are invisible to your code [[r15]].","For long calls, `AllowBackgroundResponses` returns a continuation token you can poll or use to reconnect a stream [[r15]]."]}]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview and GA labels, verified 2026-10-13","md":"The Foundry `a2a` tool and A2A 1.0 are generally available. `a2a_preview` and A2A 0.3 are in preview. The Agent Framework A2A packages are prerelease. Say the label and the date whenever you recommend one [[r16]] [[r17]] [[r14]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-l05-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A Foundry agent task receives writes on day 0, day 20 and day 70 and none afterward. Counting from day 0, on which day is retention scheduled to end?","answer":130,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"day","hint":"Foundry retains tasks and contexts for 60 days from the most recent write","explain":"Each write resets the 60-day clock. The last write was on day 70, so retention ends on day 130. Counting from day 0 or day 20 is wrong because those writes were superseded.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w09-l05-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A client calls a Foundry agent's A2A endpoint with no version header and receives 0.3-shaped payloads that its 1.0 parser rejects. What is the fix?","options":["Switch the binding to gRPC, which always returns 1.0","Enable streaming on the agent so it upgrades the protocol","Disable the Entra token check on the agent so the request is routed to the 1.0 handler instead of the default one","Send `A2A-Version: 1.0`, or use the v1.0 agent card URL, so Foundry serves 1.0"],"answer":3,"explain":"With no selector Foundry serves preview 0.3. Version 1.0 is chosen by header, query value or the version-specific card URL. For v1.0 Foundry supports only JSON-RPC and no streaming, and authentication is unrelated to the version.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w09-l05-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"A task enters `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` before a destructive step. The client obtains a token and sends the next message. What may the agent assume?","options":["That the state transition itself authorized the destructive step","That any credential received in this state authorizes every later message on the task","Nothing automatically: the agent must check that the credential and scope cover that specific operation","That the client's earlier authentication is void and the task must restart"],"answer":2,"explain":"The spec says agents MUST NOT treat the `AUTH_REQUIRED` transition as authorization, and a credential obtained then MUST NOT be assumed to cover later messages unless the implementation, issuer or an extension defines that. Restarting is not required.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l05-q4","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Microsoft A2A item to what it is.","pairs":[{"l":"Foundry `a2a` tool type","r":"Calls remote agents using A2A 1.0, generally available"},{"l":"Foundry `a2a_preview` tool type","r":"Targets A2A 0.3, in preview"},{"l":"Incoming A2A on a Foundry agent (v1.0)","r":"JSON-RPC only, text only, no streaming, Entra auth"},{"l":"`A2AExecutor` in Agent Framework","r":"Python server-side adapter built on the A2A SDK"}],"explain":"These pairings come from the Foundry and Agent Framework docs verified on 2026-10-13. The Foundry incoming limits apply to v1.0, and the Agent Framework packages are prerelease.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r8","r12","r13","r14","r15","r16","r17","r21"],"takeaways":["A2A leaves identity to HTTP: authenticate every request, scope every task operation to the caller and keep identity out of the payload.","Pick the scheme by caller location, whose authority the call carries and caller count.","`AUTH_REQUIRED` hands a credential job to the client. The state itself authorizes nothing.","Foundry's `a2a` tool is GA for 1.0. Incoming A2A is JSON-RPC, text only and non-streaming, and defaults to 0.3 without a version selector.","Agent Framework A2A packages are prerelease. `A2AAgent` hides the remote agent's tools."]},{"id":"w09-l06","order":6,"title":"Boss: connect agents you do not own","kind":"boss","minutes":22,"brief":"An interviewer asks you to connect agents owned by different teams and vendors so they can work together on customer requests. Nothing else is specified. Run the Dial-and-Defend method: clarify, list options, choose, name failure modes, then metrics and rollout.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Before you answer","level":2,"id":"before-you-answer"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The prompt","known":["Agents are owned by different teams and by outside vendors","They must cooperate on customer requests","Vendors may run different frameworks and A2A versions","The interviewer will not volunteer constraints"],"ask":["Which boundaries are crossed: same process, other teams, other organizations?","How long do tasks run, and do they need questions or artifacts?","Who is the caller, and must the callee see the end user?","Can callees reach us by webhook, or must we poll or stream?","Which versions and platforms do vendors support?"],"tension":"Opaque peers keep teams independent, but every hop adds latency, a trust decision and a version to manage."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-l06-d1","title":"A reference shape","alt":"A user talks to an orchestrator agent. The orchestrator looks up cards in a registry and calls a team agent and a vendor agent through a gateway using A2A. The identity provider feeds the gateway. The team agent uses MCP for its tools. Trace context flows to a tracing store.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> O[Orchestrator agent]\n  O -->|A2A| G[Gateway]\n  G -->|A2A| T[Team agent]\n  G -->|A2A| V[Vendor agent]\n  R[Registry] -.-> O\n  I[Identity provider] -.-> G\n  T -->|MCP| X[Team tools]\n  O -->|trace context| OBS[Tracing]","caption":"Decide where discovery, identity, policy and tracing live before you draw the agents."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where each step of the answer draws on this world","head":["Step","What you say","Levels"],"rows":[["1. Clarify the dials","Boundaries, task length, caller and delegation, reachability, versions","1, 4, 5"],["2. List the options","In-process tools, MCP wrappers, direct A2A peers, A2A through a gateway and registry","1, 2"],["3. Pick and say what flips it","Choose for the stated dials. Name the dial that would change the pick","1, 2, 4, 5"],["4. Failure modes and detection","Card swaps, SSRF in webhooks, version drift, terminal-task follow-ups, cross-tenant leaks, AUTH_REQUIRED misuse","2, 3, 4, 5"],["5. Metrics and rollout","Task success, time to first status, version mix, auth and webhook failures","3, 4, 5"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What gets points","md":"Draw the boundary before the protocol. Say which delivery mode you chose and why. Mention version negotiation and the Foundry v1.0 limits if the callee runs there. State that the card is a claim and every request is authenticated."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-l06-q1","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every element a strong cross-team A2A design includes.","options":["Check the card's `capabilities` before using streaming or push","Send `A2A-Version` on every request","Treat `contextId` as proof that the caller may read the task","Scope `GetTask` and `ListTasks` to the authenticated caller","Embed the vendor's API key in the public card for convenience"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Capability checks, version headers and per-caller scoping are in the spec. `contextId` is an opaque grouping value and not an authorization proof. Secrets never belong in a public card.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l06-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps of a first call to a vendor's agent in order.","items":["Fetch the Agent Card and verify TLS and any signature","Choose the first supported interface and the `A2A-Version`","Authenticate with the scheme the card declares","Send `SendMessage` with `returnImmediately` true, then poll or subscribe","Handle `INPUT_REQUIRED` or `AUTH_REQUIRED`, then read the Artifacts"],"explain":"You cannot choose an interface before you have read the card, and you cannot authenticate before you know the declared scheme. Returning immediately avoids blocking, and interrupted states and artifacts come after the task is running.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w09-l06-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"The vendor's agent is hosted on Foundry and exposes A2A 1.0 over JSON-RPC only, text only, with no streaming. You planned to show live progress. What changes?","options":["Use gRPC streaming instead, because it is always available","Ask the vendor to add streaming through the agent card alone","Fall back to `returnImmediately` and poll `GetTask`, showing status messages as progress","Switch to the HTTP+JSON binding, because Foundry streams events over it even for v1.0 agents"],"answer":2,"explain":"For v1.0 Foundry supports only JSON-RPC, text only and no streaming responses. Streaming over gRPC or HTTP+JSON is not available there, and a card field cannot add a capability the platform lacks. Polling works with every binding.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w09-l06-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Twelve internal teams and three vendors will expose agents. Tasks run about 15 minutes, some need questions, and governance is strict. Which design fits best?","options":["Every agent rewritten and wrapped as an in-process tool inside one monolith that all twelve teams and the vendors must deploy together","A2A peers discovered through a private registry and called through a gateway with central auth, rate limits and tracing","A single shared MCP server that every team's agent calls with no task support","Email-based hand-offs with agent summaries attached"],"answer":1,"explain":"Many owners, long tasks and strict governance point to A2A with a registry and a gateway. A monolith cannot cross vendor boundaries. An MCP server without tasks cannot carry questions or 15-minute work, and email has no contract or audit.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r8"}],"defend":{"prompt":"Connect agents owned by different teams and by outside vendors so they can work together on customer requests. Walk me through your options and tell me what you would choose.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Which boundaries do the agents cross: one process, other teams in the company, other organizations?","How long do tasks run, and do they need mid-task questions or file artifacts?","Who is the caller, and must the callee see the end user's identity?","Can callees reach us by webhook, or must we poll or stream?","Which platforms and A2A versions do the vendors run?","How strict are governance, audit and data-residency needs?"],"options":[{"name":"Agents as in-process tools","when":"One team and one process own both agents.","tradeoff":"Simplest and fastest, but it cannot cross owners or release cycles."},{"name":"Agents exposed as MCP tools","when":"The agent behaves like a stateless function with a fixed input and output.","tradeoff":"Reuses MCP hosts and auth, but a tool call has no task lifecycle unless the Tasks extension is supported on both sides."},{"name":"Direct A2A peers with pinned cards","when":"A few known partners and slow-changing cards.","tradeoff":"Low setup and no registry, but every change needs reconfiguration and policy lives in each agent."},{"name":"A2A through a gateway and private registry","when":"Many owners, strict governance and cross-boundary tasks.","tradeoff":"Central policy, discovery and tracing, at the cost of running a registry and gateway. The spec defines no registry API."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify.** I would ask which boundaries are crossed, how long tasks run and whether they need questions or artifacts, who the caller is and whether the callee must see the user, whether callees can receive webhooks, and which A2A versions and platforms vendors run. I will assume twelve internal teams and three vendors, tasks of about fifteen minutes with occasional questions, strict governance, some user-delegated calls, and a vendor on Foundry."},{"t":"table","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when"],"rows":[["In-process tools","One team and process","Separate owners or release cycles"],["MCP-wrapped agents","Stateless, fixed-shape calls","Long, interactive or artifact-producing work"],["Direct A2A with pinned cards","A few stable partners","Many agents or frequent card changes"],["A2A via gateway and registry","Many owners and strict policy","A tiny estate that cannot run the extra pieces"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Choose, and say what flips it.** Inside each team I keep agents as in-process tools. Across teams and vendors I use A2A through an API management gateway, with a private registry for discovery and signed cards where vendors publish them. Callers send `A2A-Version` on every request and check `capabilities` first. I default to `returnImmediately` with polling, use streaming where the callee supports it, and use push only for reachable server callers with validated webhooks. Service-to-service calls use client credentials or OpenID Connect, and user-delegated calls use an on-behalf-of token so the callee can scope to that user. For the Foundry vendor I use the GA `a2a` tool, select 1.0 explicitly, and accept polling because v1.0 there has no streaming."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Flip to in-process tools if one team ends up owning both sides.","Flip to MCP-wrapped agents if the work is stateless with a fixed schema and MCP hosts already exist.","Flip to pinned cards if there are only a few partners and cards rarely change.","Flip to dual-version clients if a vendor is stuck on 0.3, and plan the move to 1.0."]},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Failure modes and detection.** A swapped card: verify TLS and signatures and alert when interface URLs change. SSRF through webhooks: validate URLs and use allowlists. Version drift: log the version per call and test against both. Follow-ups on terminal tasks: create new tasks with `referenceTaskIds`. Cross-tenant leaks: scope task operations to the caller. `AUTH_REQUIRED` misuse: check the scope of each operation after a credential arrives. Lost context: Foundry keeps tasks 60 days from the last write, so persist our own summary. Trace gaps: propagate W3C trace context and log `taskId`."},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** Track task success rate per skill, time to first status, p95 task duration, the share of tasks that hit `INPUT_REQUIRED`, timeout rate, request share per protocol version, auth failures and webhook delivery success. Roll out with one vendor behind contract tests on the card, run shadow tasks next to the current process, then open the registry to more teams."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","md":"Close with the dial that would change your pick. It shows you chose, not guessed."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about boundaries, task length, caller identity, reachability and versions before choosing","w":3},{"pt":"Compares at least three real options including in-process tools","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses for stated dials and names what would flip the choice","w":3},{"pt":"Explains the card as a claim, the interface and version choice, and per-request authentication","w":3},{"pt":"Picks a delivery mode with reasons: polling, streaming or push, and knows `returnImmediately`","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with detection: card swaps, SSRF, version drift, terminal tasks, AUTH_REQUIRED misuse","w":2},{"pt":"Knows the Microsoft specifics and labels GA versus preview with a date","w":2},{"pt":"Names concrete metrics and a staged rollout","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"A vendor's agent only speaks A2A 0.3. What do you do?","a":"Send `A2A-Version: 0.3` to that callee, keep a compatibility layer in the client, and track it as debt. Remember the enum, discriminator and operation-name changes. In Foundry the `a2a_preview` tool targets 0.3 and is in preview, so ask the vendor for a 1.0 date."},{"q":"How do you give a callee the real end user's identity?","a":"A2A payloads carry no identity, so use an on-behalf-of token at the HTTP layer with a scheme the card declares, such as OAuth 2.0 authorization code or OpenID Connect. The callee authorizes the user's scope on every operation."},{"q":"A task asks for credentials halfway. How do you handle it?","a":"The task moves to `AUTH_REQUIRED`. Prefer an out-of-band channel for the credential, keep a stream or poll open to catch the resume, and never assume the credential covers later operations beyond what the agent defines."},{"q":"Why not wrap every agent as an MCP tool?","a":"A tool call has no task lifecycle unless the Tasks extension is supported on both sides. A2A has tasks, contexts, input-required and artifacts built in, so it fits long, interactive work across owners."}],"redFlags":["Treats a remote agent like a synchronous function with no timeout or task handling","Trusts the Agent Card or its skills as a security boundary","Puts secrets or sensitive internal URLs in a public card","Ignores version negotiation and assumes every peer speaks 1.0","Accepts webhook URLs without validation"]},"refs":["r1","r8","r17"],"takeaways":["Open with the dials: boundaries, task length, caller identity, reachability and versions.","Keep same-team agents in-process, and use A2A with a registry and gateway across owners.","A card is a claim. Authenticate every request, scope every task operation and verify signatures where they exist.","Default to returnImmediately with polling. Use streaming and push only where both sides support them.","Label GA and preview with a date, and name the one dial that would flip your choice."]}],"topics":[{"id":"w09-t01","order":1,"group":"Concepts","title":"Why A2A and opaque agents","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A2A is an open protocol that lets independent agents, built on different stacks and owned by different teams, discover each other, exchange tasks and return results without exposing prompts, tools or memory.","useIf":"Another team, vendor or framework owns the agent you need and you can only observe its inputs and outputs.","avoidIf":"You own both agents in one process and one team, where an in-process tool call is simpler and faster.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Without a shared protocol, every pair of agents needs a custom integration. The usual workaround is to wrap the other agent as a tool, the way MCP exposes tools. A2A's own documentation calls that inefficient: agents are built to negotiate, so squeezing them into a function signature limits what they can do. A2A exposes agents **as they are**, with no wrapping [[tr1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The central idea is **opaque execution**. The caller sees the callee's Agent Card (skills, interfaces, security) and the messages, tasks and artifacts they exchange. It never sees prompts, plans, tool implementations or memory [[tr2]] [[r1]]. Opacity is a constraint and a benefit: the owner can swap models, tools or internals without breaking callers, but the caller cannot inspect or debug the inside, so contracts (declared skills, test prompts, service levels) carry the weight."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-t01-d1","title":"What the caller can and cannot see","alt":"A user talks to a client agent. The client agent talks to a remote agent over A2A. The remote agent's prompts, tools and memory are hidden from the caller.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> C[Client agent]\n  C -->|A2A over HTTP| R[Remote agent]\n  R -.->|hidden from caller| H[Prompts, tools, memory]"},{"t":"p","md":"A2A was created by Google in April 2025. The Linux Foundation launched the A2A project on 2025-06-23, citing more than 100 supporting companies [[r9]]. Version 1.0.0 was released on 2026-03-12 and the patch 1.0.1 on 2026-05-28 [[r4]]. The specification page header still reads 1.0.0 as the latest released version (checked 2026-10-13) [[r1]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Opacity cuts both ways","md":"You gain independent release cycles and a clean trust boundary. You lose the ability to read the callee's reasoning, so plan debugging around task ids, context ids and trace context on both sides [[r8]]."}],"whereToUse":["A partner or vendor agent you call over HTTPS without access to its code or model","A platform team's specialist agent (for example a compliance reviewer) consumed by many product teams","Agents written in different languages or frameworks that release on different schedules","Specialist work that runs long and needs a task handle, progress and artifacts","Cross-tenant or cross-cloud agent calls that already sit behind an identity boundary"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You cross a service, team or organization boundary","You need a task lifecycle (long-running, input-required, artifacts) rather than one return value","The callee must stay free to change its internals without a coordinated release"],"avoid":["Both agents live in one process and belong to one team","The callee is a stateless function with a fixed schema, where a plain tool or API is simpler","You must see or control the callee's tool choices and reasoning, which opacity prevents"],"flips":["The callee must be inspectable: pull it into your process or expose it as an MCP server you control","The per-hop latency budget is under a second: co-locate the agents and call in process","Several partners already speak A2A: adopt the standard even for simple skills instead of one more bespoke API"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["A2A peer (this topic)","Cross-boundary, opaque, long-running or multi-turn work","Same process and team","Network hop, versioning, auth and retry work"],["Agent as in-process tool","One process and one team","Independent release cycles or a trust boundary","Tight coupling, shared deployment"],["Agent wrapped as an MCP tool","The host only speaks MCP and calls are short","Long, multi-turn work with artifacts (no task lifecycle unless the Tasks extension is used)","Loses context grouping and artifact semantics"],["Bespoke REST API","One partner and a fixed schema","Many partners or many agents","A custom client and contract per partner"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A wrong answer is traced to the remote agent's front door and the trail ends","cause":"Opaque execution hides internals by design, and no trace context was shared","fix":"Log `taskId`, `contextId` and trace context on both sides, agree on test prompts per skill, and ask the owner for traces keyed by `taskId` [[r8]]"},{"symptom":"Some clients fail to parse replies from a newer agent","cause":"A2A v1.0 changed enum casing, removed the `kind` discriminator and renamed operations, and v0.3 clients still exist [[r2]]","fix":"Send the `A2A-Version` header, read `protocolVersion` per interface in the card, and plan a dual-version period"},{"symptom":"Latency doubles after two agents in one repo are split into services","cause":"A2A was adopted where no boundary exists, so every call pays a network hop [[r12]]","fix":"Call co-owned agents as in-process tools and keep A2A for real boundaries"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t01-s1","title":"A supplier's agent across a company boundary","situation":"A retailer's assistant needs quotes from a supplier's agent. The supplier uses another stack, will not share prompts, and jobs take from 30 seconds to a few hours. Several suppliers will follow.","constraints":["Different organization and opaque callee","Long-running jobs with possible clarifying questions","More partners expected over time"],"unknowns":["Do suppliers already publish A2A Agent Cards?","How does each supplier authenticate callers?","Do jobs ever need human approval mid-way?"],"question":"How do you integrate the supplier agents?","options":[{"name":"A2A peer calls","verdict":"best","why":"The boundary, opacity and long-running work are what A2A models: Agent Card discovery, tasks, artifacts and input-required turns.","tradeoff":"You own version negotiation, auth per supplier and distributed-call failure handling."},{"name":"Bespoke REST API per supplier","verdict":"good","why":"Works for one partner with a fixed schema and gives full control of the contract.","tradeoff":"Every new supplier repeats the client, auth and polling design."},{"name":"Wrap each supplier as an MCP tool","verdict":"good","why":"Fine if the host only speaks MCP and the Tasks extension covers long jobs.","tradeoff":"You give up Agent Card discovery, context grouping and A2A's artifact model, and tie partners to MCP."},{"name":"Import the supplier's logic into your process","verdict":"poor","why":"The supplier will not share prompts or code, and a copy would drift from their live behaviour.","tradeoff":"Right only when you own both sides and want to merge them."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only one supplier, fixed schema, jobs under 2 seconds","then":"A plain REST call is simpler than A2A."},{"if":"The supplier already publishes an MCP server and jobs are short","then":"Use the MCP tool and skip A2A."}],"takeaway":"Cross-organization, opaque and long-running work is the A2A sweet spot."},{"id":"w09-t01-s2","title":"Two agents in one service with a tight latency budget","situation":"A planner agent calls a formatter agent maintained by the same team in the same repository. The user-facing budget is 400 ms and both agents deploy together.","constraints":["Same team, same process, same release","Tight latency budget","Low risk, internal data only"],"unknowns":["Does the formatter ever need to scale or release independently?","Is the formatter reused by other teams?"],"question":"How should the planner call the formatter?","options":[{"name":"In-process agent as a tool","verdict":"best","why":"No network hop, no protocol, one deployment, and the framework handles the call.","tradeoff":"Tight coupling: a change to the formatter ships with the planner."},{"name":"A2A over loopback HTTP","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds serialization, a hop and auth work with no boundary to justify them.","tradeoff":"Right once the formatter moves to another team or service."},{"name":"Merge both into one agent","verdict":"good","why":"Removes the hop entirely and simplifies tracing.","tradeoff":"A larger prompt and tool surface, and a harder-to-test single agent."}],"flips":[{"if":"Another team starts consuming the formatter","then":"Expose it over A2A so it can release independently."},{"if":"The formatter needs its own scaling or GPU pool","then":"Split it into a service and use A2A or a plain API."}],"takeaway":"Without a boundary, the simplest call wins."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does opaque execution mean for an A2A client?","options":["The client must reimplement the remote agent's skills locally","The client sees the remote agent's tool calls but not its final answers","The client sees the Agent Card and the exchanged messages, tasks and artifacts, but not the remote agent's prompts, tools or memory","The remote agent encrypts every message so only the model can read it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Opaque execution means collaboration through declared capabilities and exchanged content only. Tool calls stay hidden, opacity is about visibility and not encryption, and nothing is reimplemented locally."},{"id":"w09-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which situations favour A2A over calling the other agent as an in-process tool? Select all that apply.","options":["The other agent belongs to a different organization","The other agent must release on its own schedule","The work needs a task handle, progress and artifacts","You want to read the other agent's prompts while debugging"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Boundary, independent release and a task lifecycle are the reasons to use A2A. Reading the other agent's prompts is the opposite of opacity, so that need points to in-process composition or a system you control."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What problem does A2A solve that wrapping an agent as a tool does not?","back":"It exposes agents as they are, with tasks, context, artifacts and discovery, instead of reducing them to a function signature."},{"front":"What does a caller see of a remote A2A agent?","back":"The Agent Card plus the messages, tasks and artifacts exchanged. Prompts, tools and memory stay hidden."},{"front":"When was A2A v1.0.0 released, and who governs the project?","back":"2026-03-12. The Linux Foundation launched the A2A project on 2025-06-23."}],"related":["w09-t02","w09-t03","w09-l01","w08-t01","w11-t25","w12-t11"],"refs":["r1","r2","r4","r8","r9","r12","tr1","tr2"]},{"id":"w09-t02","order":2,"group":"Concepts","title":"A2A vs MCP vs agent-as-tool","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Three ways to use another agent's work: call it as an in-process function, reach it as an MCP tool, or talk to it as an A2A peer. Ownership, task shape, trust and latency decide which.","useIf":"You must decide how one agent should reach a capability or another agent.","avoidIf":"You want one answer for the whole system: mix the three, one per boundary.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The A2A project draws the line by direction. **MCP is vertical**: it gives one agent tools and resources. **A2A is horizontal**: independent, often opaque agents discover each other, share tasks and exchange results. They complement each other: A2A between agents, MCP inside each agent [[r3]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Microsoft's Agent Framework guidance is the same in practice. Use **agents as tools** when agents share a process and a team. Use **A2A** when you cross a service, team or organization boundary, and budget for the network latency, retries and versioning that come with it [[r12]]. An MCP tool call has no task lifecycle unless the Tasks extension is supported [[r20]], while A2A has tasks, contexts and artifacts built in [[r1]]."},{"t":"dials","id":"w09-t02-pick","title":"Pick how to reach the other capability","prompt":"Flip the dials. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"owner","label":"Who owns the callee","values":[{"id":"same","label":"Same team and process"},{"id":"team","label":"Another team, same company"},{"id":"org","label":"Another organization"}]},{"id":"shape","label":"Shape of the work","values":[{"id":"short","label":"Short and stateless"},{"id":"long","label":"Long, multi-turn or artifacts"}]},{"id":"latency","label":"Latency per call","values":[{"id":"tight","label":"Tight (hundreds of ms)"},{"id":"relaxed","label":"Relaxed (seconds or more)"}]}],"options":[{"id":"tool","name":"In-process agent as tool","summary":"A function wrapper around another agent in the same runtime."},{"id":"mcp","name":"MCP tool","summary":"The callee is a tool on an MCP server."},{"id":"a2a","name":"A2A peer","summary":"The callee is an independent agent with a card and tasks."},{"id":"api","name":"Plain API call","summary":"A fixed HTTP contract wrapped as a function tool."}],"scores":{"tool":{"owner":{"same":{"s":2,"why":"No boundary to pay for."},"team":{"s":-1,"why":"Couples release cycles across teams."},"org":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot import code you do not own."}},"shape":{"short":{"s":1,"why":"A function call is enough."},"long":{"s":-1,"why":"Long work needs your own durability and state handling."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":2,"why":"No network hop."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Latency is not the constraint, so it adds nothing."}}},"mcp":{"owner":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but a protocol is unneeded overhead."},"team":{"s":1,"why":"A shared tool server serves many hosts."},"org":{"s":1,"why":"Standard auth and discovery help across companies."}},"shape":{"short":{"s":2,"why":"Tools and short calls are MCP's home ground."},"long":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs the Tasks extension and loses A2A's contexts and artifacts."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"One hop, no agent negotiation."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Fine at any latency."}}},"a2a":{"owner":{"same":{"s":-2,"why":"Protocol and hop cost with no boundary."},"team":{"s":2,"why":"Independent releases and ownership are the fit."},"org":{"s":2,"why":"Opaque execution and a trust boundary are the design goal."}},"shape":{"short":{"s":-1,"why":"Task machinery is heavier than the job."},"long":{"s":2,"why":"Tasks, input-required turns, streaming and push are built in."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"Network hop plus agent latency."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"The overhead disappears behind the work."}}},"api":{"owner":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Acceptable but needless indirection."},"team":{"s":1,"why":"A stable contract suits cross-team reuse."},"org":{"s":1,"why":"Works when the partner publishes a fixed API."}},"shape":{"short":{"s":1,"why":"A deterministic call needs no agent."},"long":{"s":-1,"why":"You rebuild polling and status yourself."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"One hop, no reasoning."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}}},"takeaway":"Cross-boundary, long-running work points to A2A. Short, stateless capabilities point to tools, MCP or plain APIs. Same-team calls point to in-process."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Answer with the boundary","md":"When asked which to use, name the ownership boundary, the task shape and the latency budget first. Short, schema-shaped capabilities are tools. Independent agents with long or conversational work are A2A peers. Same-team code stays in process."}],"whereToUse":["Choosing how a planner agent reaches a specialist agent, a data lookup or a partner service","Reviewing an architecture where every helper was wrapped as an MCP tool or every call became A2A","Setting an organization-wide default: A2A for cross-team agents, MCP for shared tool catalogs","Interview answers about the boundary between agents and tools"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use A2A when the callee is an agent with its own reasoning and state, behind a team or organization boundary","Use MCP when the callee is a capability with a schema: a tool, a resource or a prompt","Use an in-process agent tool when one team ships both agents together"],"avoid":["Avoid A2A for a deterministic lookup: the task machinery adds cost with no benefit","Avoid MCP wrappers for long, multi-turn agents unless you add the Tasks extension and accept the loss of context grouping","Avoid in-process composition across teams: release cycles and trust get entangled"],"flips":["A tool call grows from seconds to many minutes: move from MCP tool to A2A or add the Tasks extension","A co-owned helper agent gets a second consuming team: promote it from in-process tool to an A2A service","A partner already ships an MCP server and the work is short: take the MCP server and skip A2A"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["In-process agent tool","One team, one process, tight latency","Another team or organization owns the callee","Coupled releases and shared failure domain [[r13]]"],["MCP tool","Short, schema-shaped capabilities reused by many hosts","Long or multi-turn agent conversations","No task lifecycle without the Tasks extension [[r20]]"],["A2A peer","Opaque, independent agents with long-running work","Deterministic or sub-second calls","Network, auth, retries and version negotiation"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Every user request pays an extra round trip to a helper agent in the same repo","cause":"A2A was used where agents share a process and team","fix":"Wrap the helper as an in-process tool and keep A2A for real boundaries [[r12]]"},{"symptom":"A long MCP tool call loses its result when the client disconnects","cause":"A plain tool call has no task lifecycle, so there is nothing to poll or resume","fix":"Use the MCP Tasks extension if the host supports it, or expose the work as an A2A task [[r20]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t02-s1","title":"A stock lookup shared by many hosts","situation":"Many assistants need a deterministic stock lookup with a fixed schema. Calls take 80 ms and are stateless.","constraints":["Stateless, deterministic","Many consuming hosts","Tight latency"],"unknowns":["Do hosts already speak MCP?","Is the lookup also needed by non-agent systems?"],"question":"How do you expose it?","options":[{"name":"MCP tool","verdict":"best","why":"A schema-shaped capability reused by many hosts is MCP's purpose, with standard discovery and auth.","tradeoff":"Tool definitions cost context tokens in every host."},{"name":"Plain REST API with a function tool per host","verdict":"good","why":"Also serves non-agent callers and needs no MCP stack.","tradeoff":"Each host writes its own tool wrapper."},{"name":"A2A agent","verdict":"poor","why":"There is no reasoning, state or long-running work to model as tasks.","tradeoff":"Right if the lookup becomes a negotiating agent with follow-up questions."}],"flips":[{"if":"Lookups start to need clarifying questions","then":"Promote the capability to an A2A agent."},{"if":"Only one host will ever call it","then":"A function tool over REST is enough."}],"takeaway":"Deterministic and schema-shaped means tool, not peer."},{"id":"w09-t02-s2","title":"A compliance agent used by twelve product teams","situation":"A platform team runs a compliance-review agent. Product teams send drafts, the agent asks clarifying questions and may take 10 minutes. It releases weekly and the teams use different frameworks.","constraints":["Other team owns the callee","Long and multi-turn","Relaxed latency","Internal, moderately sensitive data"],"unknowns":["How do teams authenticate to the agent?","Are results needed as downloadable artifacts?"],"question":"How do product agents reach it?","options":[{"name":"A2A peer with a published Agent Card","verdict":"best","why":"Independent releases, clarifying questions through input-required, artifacts for the report and framework-neutral clients.","tradeoff":"Each team handles version negotiation, auth and retries."},{"name":"MCP tool with the Tasks extension","verdict":"good","why":"Fits if the hosts are already MCP clients and conversations stay short.","tradeoff":"Weaker support for multi-turn context and artifacts."},{"name":"Shared library imported by each team","verdict":"poor","why":"Every team must upgrade on the platform team's schedule and the agent's model and data access get duplicated.","tradeoff":"Right only for pure, deterministic rules with no model or data dependency."}],"flips":[{"if":"The review becomes a fixed rule check under 1 second","then":"Expose a plain API or MCP tool."},{"if":"The data is regulated and each call needs per-user delegation","then":"Keep A2A but design authentication and delegation first (see A2A authentication and trust)."}],"takeaway":"Another team's long, conversational agent is an A2A peer."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t02-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each approach to the situation it fits best.","pairs":[{"l":"In-process agent as tool","r":"One team ships both agents in one process"},{"l":"MCP tool","r":"Short, schema-shaped capability reused by many hosts"},{"l":"A2A peer","r":"Independent opaque agent with long-running, multi-turn work"},{"l":"Plain API call","r":"Fixed contract with no reasoning needed"}],"explain":"Each pairing follows the boundary and task shape. In-process removes protocol cost, MCP standardizes tools, A2A models independent agents and tasks, and a plain API suits a fixed contract."},{"id":"w09-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A vendor's analysis agent is wrapped as an MCP tool. Jobs take 12 minutes and calls fail when the client connection drops. What is the most direct fix?","options":["Raise the client timeout to 15 minutes","Retry the same call three times","Switch the tool to a cheaper model","Use a protocol with a task lifecycle: the MCP Tasks extension or an A2A task"],"answer":3,"explain":"The cause is that a plain tool call has no task handle to poll or resume. A longer timeout still loses the result on disconnect, blind retries repeat 12 minutes of work, and the model choice is unrelated."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does the A2A project describe the MCP and A2A split?","back":"MCP is vertical (an agent to its tools and resources). A2A is horizontal (independent agents to each other). Use A2A between agents and MCP inside each."},{"front":"When is an in-process agent tool the right call?","back":"When one team ships both agents in one process. A2A adds network latency, retries and versioning that only pay off across boundaries."},{"front":"What does a plain MCP tool call lack that an A2A task has?","back":"A task lifecycle: ids, states, progress, input-required turns and artifacts, unless the MCP Tasks extension is used."}],"related":["w09-t01","w09-t04","w09-l01","w08-t01","w08-t08","w08-t17","w11-t25"],"refs":["r1","r3","r12","r13","r20"]},{"id":"w09-t03","order":3,"group":"Protocol","title":"Agent Card and discovery","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The Agent Card is a JSON document that an A2A server publishes to describe its identity, endpoints, capabilities, skills and security. Clients fetch it from a well-known URL, a registry or direct configuration.","useIf":"You publish an agent for others to call, or you must decide how a client finds and trusts a remote agent.","avoidIf":"You treat the card as proof of identity or as a contract: it is a claim that must be verified and then authenticated against.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A server serves its card at `/.well-known/agent-card.json` (the well-known URI pattern of RFC 8615) [[r6]] [[tr3]]. In v1.0 the required fields are `name`, `description`, `supportedInterfaces`, `version`, `capabilities`, `defaultInputModes`, `defaultOutputModes` and `skills` [[r1]]. Each entry in `supportedInterfaces` has a `url`, a `protocolBinding` (`JSONRPC`, `GRPC` or `HTTP+JSON`), a `protocolVersion` and an optional `tenant`. The list is ordered and the first entry is preferred."},{"t":"table","caption":"Read each field as a claim and decide what to check [[r1]]","head":["Field","What it declares","Check before calling"],"rows":[["`supportedInterfaces`","Where and how to call","Pick a binding you support, send `tenant` exactly as declared"],["`capabilities`","`streaming`, `pushNotifications`, `extensions`, `extendedAgentCard`","A missing or false flag means the operation returns `UnsupportedOperationError`"],["`skills`","Each has `id`, `name`, `description`, `tags`","Descriptive only, not a contract: test the skill"],["`securitySchemes`","API key, HTTP, OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, mutual TLS","Obtain credentials out of band, then authenticate every request"],["`signatures`","Optional JWS over the card","Proves origin and integrity of the card, nothing about behaviour"]]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-t03-d1","title":"Finding and vetting a card","alt":"Flow from needing an agent, through a known URL or a registry query, to fetching the card, verifying it, choosing an interface and authenticating.","src":"flowchart TD\n  N[Need an agent] --> K{Know the host?}\n  K -->|yes| W[GET well-known card]\n  K -->|no| R[Query registry]\n  W --> V[Verify card and capabilities]\n  R --> V\n  V --> I[Pick first supported interface]\n  I --> A[Authenticate every request]"},{"t":"p","md":"Cards may be signed as a JWS (RFC 7515) over the RFC 8785 canonical form, excluding the `signatures` field [[r1]] [[r18]] [[r19]]. Servers SHOULD send `Cache-Control: max-age` and an `ETag`, and clients SHOULD use conditional requests [[r6]]. The spec defines no registry API, so you design that layer yourself [[r6]]. Anything sensitive belongs in the authenticated **extended card** (`GetExtendedAgentCard`, declared by `capabilities.extendedAgentCard`), not the public one."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Foundry differs from the default path","md":"A Foundry agent's card requires Microsoft Entra authentication and uses the path `agentCard/v1.0`, not `/.well-known/agent-card.json`. Anonymous card access is not supported (checked 2026-10-13) [[r17]]."}],"whereToUse":["A public agent that publishes its card at a well-known URL for open discovery","An enterprise catalog where a registry stores cards and filters them by skill or tag","A client library that resolves a card, picks an interface and builds an agent object from it","A security review that checks which schemes, scopes and interfaces a callee declares","A gateway that caches cards and refreshes them with conditional requests"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Well-known URI for agents meant for broad discovery on a domain you can reach","A curated registry when many teams publish agents and you need governance and skill search","Direct configuration for a few tightly coupled, high-trust agents"],"avoid":["Do not put internal URLs or sensitive skills in a public card","Do not follow a card's endpoint blindly when tasks will carry sensitive data","Do not skip the capability check: operations the card does not declare fail"],"flips":["Cards change weekly: honor `Cache-Control` and `ETag` and refresh with conditional requests instead of pinning forever","Cards carry sensitive detail: authenticate the card endpoint and serve an extended card or selective disclosure","Trust is low and data is sensitive: pin the host and verify the JWS signature against a key you trust"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Discovery approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Well-known URI","Open or domain-controlled discovery","You need search by skill or governance","Cards are public unless the endpoint is protected"],["Curated registry","Many teams, central policy, skill search","You have two agents and no platform team","You build and run the registry (no standard API) [[r6]]"],["Direct configuration","Static, tightly coupled agents","Cards change or agents multiply","Every change means client reconfiguration"],["Registry plus signed cards","Cross-organization, low trust","Latency or key management is a burden","Key distribution and rotation work"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Calls fail with `UnsupportedOperationError` on streaming or the extended card","cause":"The client assumed a capability the card does not declare","fix":"Read `capabilities` first and fall back to polling when `streaming` is false or missing [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Traffic flows to an unexpected host after a card refresh","cause":"The client trusted the `url` in a refreshed card without any allowlist or signature check","fix":"Allowlist hosts, verify the JWS when present and alert when an interface URL changes"},{"symptom":"Internal URLs and sensitive skills show up in a public card","cause":"Everything was placed in the unauthenticated card","fix":"Keep the public card minimal and serve detail through the authenticated extended card [[r6]]"},{"symptom":"Requests are rejected on a shared multi-agent endpoint","cause":"The `tenant` value declared on the interface was not sent","fix":"Send `tenant` exactly as declared on the selected interface [[r1]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t03-s1","title":"Forty internal agents, one platform team","situation":"Forty agents owned by different teams must be findable by skill. Security wants an audit of who can see which agent. Cards change about weekly.","constraints":["Internal, many owners","Search by skill and audit needed","Cards change weekly"],"unknowns":["Is there a platform team to run a catalog?","Do some agents hide sensitive skills?"],"question":"How do clients discover agents?","options":[{"name":"Curated registry with authenticated access and selective disclosure","verdict":"best","why":"Central governance, search by skill, per-caller visibility and one place to audit.","tradeoff":"You build and operate the registry, since the spec defines no registry API."},{"name":"Well-known URI scan across team domains","verdict":"good","why":"Standard and automatable, and each team stays in control of its card.","tradeoff":"No central search or policy, and every endpoint needs its own protection."},{"name":"Hard-coded endpoints in each client","verdict":"poor","why":"Every card change forces client redeployments across forty agents.","tradeoff":"Right for two tightly coupled agents that rarely change."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only five agents and one owning team","then":"Direct configuration is enough."},{"if":"Agents cross company boundaries","then":"Add signed cards and host pinning."}],"takeaway":"Many owners and a need to search mean a registry."},{"id":"w09-t03-s2","title":"A third-party agent that will see customer data","situation":"Your assistant will send customer records to an external agent found through a partner directory. A wrong endpoint would leak data. The card changes roughly monthly.","constraints":["Low trust, sensitive data","Card changes monthly","Direct regulatory exposure"],"unknowns":["Does the partner sign its cards?","Who approves a new endpoint?"],"question":"How much do you trust the card at runtime?","options":[{"name":"Pin host and key, verify the JWS, alert on interface changes","verdict":"best","why":"A card refresh cannot silently redirect customer data, and signatures prove the card's origin.","tradeoff":"Key rotation and change approval take operational effort."},{"name":"Fetch the well-known card on every call and follow it","verdict":"good","why":"Always current and simple.","tradeoff":"Whoever controls DNS or the host controls where data goes."},{"name":"Follow any card URL the model discovers","verdict":"poor","why":"A prompt-injected or malicious card could redirect tasks and data anywhere.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only in a sandbox with synthetic data."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data is public and low risk","then":"Fetch with caching and skip pinning."},{"if":"Partner cannot sign cards","then":"Pin the host and TLS identity and review each card change by hand."}],"takeaway":"The card is a claim. Pin what you cannot afford to be wrong about."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t03-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps of using a remote agent in a sensible order.","items":["Fetch the Agent Card","Check capabilities and the supported interfaces","Select the first interface whose binding you support","Obtain credentials out of band for the declared scheme","Send the authenticated request, with `tenant` if declared"],"explain":"You learn what the agent supports before choosing an interface, and credentials depend on the declared scheme. Authentication still happens on every request, not once at discovery."},{"id":"w09-t03-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which fields are required in a v1.0 Agent Card? Select all that apply.","options":["`supportedInterfaces`","`skills`","`provider`","`signatures`"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"`supportedInterfaces` and `skills` are required, along with name, description, version, capabilities and the default input and output modes. `provider` and `signatures` are optional."},{"id":"w09-t03-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"A card carries a valid JWS signature. What does that prove?","options":["The agent is safe to send sensitive data to","The listed skills behave as described","The card was published by the key holder and was not modified","The agent will accept any credential"],"answer":2,"explain":"A signature establishes origin and integrity of the card document. It says nothing about the agent's behaviour, safety or authentication policy."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Where does an A2A server publish its Agent Card by default?","back":"At `/.well-known/agent-card.json` on its domain. Foundry agents use an authenticated custom path, `agentCard/v1.0`."},{"front":"Which Agent Card fields tell a client which operations are available?","back":"`capabilities` (`streaming`, `pushNotifications`, `extensions`, `extendedAgentCard`). A missing flag means the operation returns `UnsupportedOperationError`."},{"front":"How is an Agent Card signed?","back":"As a JWS (RFC 7515) over the RFC 8785 canonical form of the card, excluding the `signatures` field."},{"front":"Where should sensitive card detail live?","back":"In the authenticated extended card (`GetExtendedAgentCard`), not the public card."}],"related":["w09-t01","w09-t06","w09-t09","w09-l02","w12-t13"],"refs":["r1","r6","r17","r18","r19","tr3"]},{"id":"w09-t04","order":4,"group":"Protocol","title":"Task lifecycle and states","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A Task is the stateful unit of work in A2A. It moves through defined states, ends in one of four terminal states or pauses in an interrupted state that waits for the client.","useIf":"A remote agent's work takes longer than one reply or may need input, so callers need a handle to track it.","avoidIf":"The agent only returns quick, self-contained answers: reply with a direct Message and skip task state.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"When an agent receives a message it either replies with a stateless `Message` or starts a `Task` [[r5]]. A task has a server-generated `id`, a `contextId` that groups related tasks and messages, a `status` (state, optional message, timestamp), `artifacts` and `history` [[r1]]. Clients cannot choose a new `taskId`."},{"t":"p","md":"The spec lists nine `TaskState` values: `UNSPECIFIED`, `SUBMITTED`, `WORKING`, four **terminal** states (`COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED`, `REJECTED`) and two **interrupted** states (`INPUT_REQUIRED`, `AUTH_REQUIRED`) [[r1]]. A terminal task is immutable: it cannot restart, so a refinement starts a new task in the same `contextId`, optionally pointing at the old one with `referenceTaskIds` [[r5]]. An interrupted task waits: the client replies with a message carrying the same `taskId` and `contextId`, and mismatched ids are rejected [[r1]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-t04-d1","title":"Task states and who moves them","alt":"A task starts submitted, becomes working, may pause as input required or auth required and return to working, and ends as completed, failed or canceled. It can also be rejected at the start.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> SUBMITTED\n  SUBMITTED --> WORKING\n  SUBMITTED --> REJECTED\n  WORKING --> INPUT_REQUIRED\n  INPUT_REQUIRED --> WORKING: client replies\n  WORKING --> AUTH_REQUIRED\n  AUTH_REQUIRED --> WORKING: credential obtained\n  WORKING --> COMPLETED\n  WORKING --> FAILED\n  WORKING --> CANCELED\n  COMPLETED --> [*]"},{"t":"p","md":"`SendMessage` may return either a Task or a direct Message. Agents come in three styles: **message-only** (always replies with messages), **task-generating** (always a task, even for simple answers) and **hybrid** (messages to agree scope, then a task for committed work) [[r5]]. `CancelTask` on a task that is not cancelable returns `TaskNotCancelableError`, and sending a message to a terminal task returns `UnsupportedOperationError` [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["A remote analyst agent whose jobs run from seconds to hours","A workflow step that may stop to ask a human a question before continuing","A client UI that must show progress, let users cancel and resume later","An orchestrator that tracks several parallel follow-up tasks inside one conversation context","Audit trails that need a clean mapping from each request to its result"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use a Task when the work is long, trackable or may pause for input or authorization","Use a hybrid agent when simple questions should stay cheap but real work needs a handle","Use `referenceTaskIds` and a new task for refinements, never a restarted task"],"avoid":["Do not return tasks for trivial one-shot answers if callers would rather get a Message","Do not send follow-up messages to a terminal task","Do not treat an interrupted state as failure or as completion"],"flips":["Latency matters more than traceability for simple calls: a message-only agent avoids creating a task per request","Every call must be auditable and cancelable: a task-generating agent gives each one an id","Callers need conversational continuity across tasks: pass the same `contextId`"]},"alternatives":{"title":"How an agent can respond","head":["Style","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Message-only agent","Quick answers, simple tools","Work is long or needs input-required turns","No handle to cancel, poll or audit"],["Task-generating agent","Everything must be tracked and cancelable","Most calls are trivial","A completed task object for every simple call"],["Hybrid agent","Quick questions plus occasional real jobs","Clients cannot handle both reply types","Callers must branch on Task or Message"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A follow-up returns `UnsupportedOperationError`","cause":"The message targeted a task that is already terminal","fix":"Start a new task in the same `contextId` and set `referenceTaskIds` to the earlier task [[r5]]"},{"symptom":"A polling loop never ends or the UI reports a failure while the agent waits","cause":"The client only watched for terminal states and ignored `INPUT_REQUIRED` and `AUTH_REQUIRED`","fix":"Treat interrupted states as a call to act: reply on the same `taskId` and `contextId`"},{"symptom":"Requests with client-made task ids are rejected","cause":"Task ids are server-generated and client-provided ids for new tasks are not supported","fix":"Send a message without a `taskId` and use the id the server returns [[r1]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t04-s1","title":"Designing the reply style of a mixed workload agent","situation":"A remote agent answers many quick questions and occasionally runs 20-minute analyses that need clarifying questions. Callers include a chat UI and batch jobs.","constraints":["Mixed short and long work","Needs input-required turns","Chat UI wants low overhead for short answers"],"unknowns":["Do callers handle both Task and Message replies?","Must short calls be auditable too?"],"question":"Which reply style do you implement?","options":[{"name":"Hybrid: messages for scoping and quick answers, a task for committed work","verdict":"best","why":"Cheap short calls and a full lifecycle for the long jobs.","tradeoff":"Callers must branch on Task versus Message."},{"name":"Task-generating: always return a task","verdict":"good","why":"One uniform client path and every call is trackable.","tradeoff":"Every quick question creates a completed task object."},{"name":"Message-only","verdict":"poor","why":"There is no handle for the 20-minute work and no input-required state for clarifications.","tradeoff":"Right for a plain Q and A agent with no long jobs."}],"flips":[{"if":"Auditors require a record for every call","then":"Switch to task-generating."},{"if":"The long jobs disappear","then":"Message-only is simplest."}],"takeaway":"Match the reply style to how much of the work needs a handle."},{"id":"w09-t04-s2","title":"INPUT_REQUIRED and the human is away for two days","situation":"A task enters `INPUT_REQUIRED` asking which budget line to charge. The responsible person returns in two days. The remote agent keeps tasks for at least a week.","constraints":["Interrupted state with a slow human","Cost of a restart is high","Wrong answer has financial impact"],"unknowns":["How long does the remote agent keep an interrupted task?","Can the question be answered by a policy?"],"question":"What does the client do?","options":[{"name":"Surface the question, keep the task open and reply on the same `taskId` and `contextId`","verdict":"best","why":"It preserves the agent's progress and context and lets a human answer.","tradeoff":"The client must persist task ids and handle retention limits."},{"name":"Cancel and start a new task with the answer included later","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and avoids long-lived state.","tradeoff":"Loses the agent's partial work and restarts costs."},{"name":"Let an LLM guess the budget line","verdict":"poor","why":"A guess on a financial decision defeats the reason the agent asked.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only where policy defines a safe default."}],"flips":[{"if":"Retention at the remote agent is shorter than the wait","then":"Cancel and restart with the answer in the first message."},{"if":"A written policy answers the question","then":"Auto-reply from the policy and log it."}],"takeaway":"An interrupted task is a pause, not an error."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"How many of the `TaskState` values in the v1.0 specification are terminal?","answer":4,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"states","hint":"Count the states after which a task cannot restart.","explain":"The terminal states are `COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED` and `REJECTED`. `INPUT_REQUIRED` and `AUTH_REQUIRED` are interrupted, and `UNSPECIFIED`, `SUBMITTED` and `WORKING` are not terminal."},{"id":"w09-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A client sends a refinement to a task that is `COMPLETED` and gets `UnsupportedOperationError`. What is the correct approach?","options":["Resend the message until the task reopens","Create a new task in the same `contextId` and set `referenceTaskIds` to the old task","Send the message with a new random `taskId`","Cancel the completed task first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Terminal tasks are immutable, so refinements start new tasks in the same context. Retrying never reopens a task, client-made task ids are not supported, and a completed task cannot be canceled."},{"id":"w09-t04-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each state to what it means for the task.","pairs":[{"l":"`COMPLETED`","r":"Terminal: the task ended and cannot restart"},{"l":"`INPUT_REQUIRED`","r":"Interrupted: waits for the client's reply"},{"l":"`WORKING`","r":"In progress: the agent is processing"},{"l":"`AUTH_REQUIRED`","r":"Interrupted: waits for a credential"}],"explain":"Terminal states end the task. Interrupted states wait for the client: `INPUT_REQUIRED` for a reply and `AUTH_REQUIRED` for a credential. `WORKING` is the active state between them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the four terminal and two interrupted A2A task states.","back":"Terminal: `COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED`, `REJECTED`. Interrupted: `INPUT_REQUIRED`, `AUTH_REQUIRED`."},{"front":"How do you refine the result of a completed A2A task?","back":"Start a new task in the same `contextId`, optionally with `referenceTaskIds` pointing at the earlier task. Terminal tasks cannot restart."},{"front":"Who generates `taskId` and `contextId`?","back":"The server. Clients echo them on follow-ups and cannot choose a new `taskId`. Mismatched ids are rejected."}],"related":["w09-t05","w09-t07","w09-t08","w09-l03","w11-t24"],"refs":["r1","r5"]},{"id":"w09-t05","order":5,"group":"Protocol","title":"Message, Part and Artifact","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A Message carries communication between client and agent, a Part is the typed content container inside messages and artifacts, and an Artifact is a deliverable produced by a task.","useIf":"You design what a remote agent sends back and must decide what goes in a message, what goes in an artifact and which part type carries it.","avoidIf":"You put task results in status messages: the spec says messages are not a reliable delivery mechanism.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A `Message` has a `messageId`, a `role` (`ROLE_USER` or `ROLE_AGENT`), `parts`, and optional `taskId`, `contextId` and `referenceTaskIds`. A `Part` holds exactly one of `text`, `raw` (inline bytes, base64 in JSON), `url` (a reference) or `data` (any structured JSON value), plus `mediaType`, `filename` and `metadata`. An `Artifact` has an `artifactId`, optional `name` and `description`, and one or more parts [[r1]] [[tr2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The spec separates **communication from output**. Messages carry instructions, questions, clarifications and status. Results SHOULD be returned as artifacts of a task. Not every message is persisted in task history, and a client that reconnects to a stream may miss status messages, so messages must not carry critical information [[r1]]. Streamed artifacts can arrive in chunks: `append` extends an earlier artifact with the same id and `lastChunk` marks the end [[r1]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"A completed task result: one artifact with two parts","code":"{\n  \"id\": \"task-7\",\n  \"contextId\": \"ctx-1\",\n  \"status\": { \"state\": \"TASK_STATE_COMPLETED\" },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"artifactId\": \"quote-1\",\n      \"name\": \"Quote\",\n      \"parts\": [\n        { \"text\": \"Quote ready for part 12345.\", \"mediaType\": \"text/plain\" },\n        { \"data\": { \"price\": 41.5, \"currency\": \"EUR\", \"leadDays\": 3 }, \"mediaType\": \"application/json\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}","caption":"Illustrative shape based on the v1.0 data model, not a captured response."},{"t":"table","caption":"Choosing a Part type [[r1]]","head":["Part field","Carries","Good for"],"rows":[["`text`","Plain text","Answers, summaries, short explanations"],["`data`","Structured JSON","Forms, quotes and results a program consumes"],["`url`","A reference to content","Large or protected files fetched separately"],["`raw`","Inline bytes (base64 in JSON)","Small images or files where one round trip matters"]]},{"t":"p","md":"The card's `defaultInputModes` and `defaultOutputModes` list the media types an agent accepts and produces, and an unsupported part type returns `ContentTypeNotSupportedError`. Foundry's incoming A2A endpoint supports only the **text** modality (checked 2026-10-13) [[r17]]."}],"whereToUse":["Returning a generated report, image or dataset as a task artifact","Passing structured input such as a form or a parts list to a remote agent as a `data` part","Sending clarifying questions with `INPUT_REQUIRED` as a status message","Referencing large files by `url` so protocol payloads stay small","Declaring supported media types on the Agent Card so clients can negotiate"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Put results in artifacts and keep messages for questions, scoping and progress","Use `data` parts with `application/json` when a program consumes the result","Use `url` parts for large or access-controlled files"],"avoid":["Do not hide the only copy of a result in a status message","Do not inline large files as base64 in streamed events","Do not omit `mediaType`: receivers should not have to guess"],"flips":["Peer is a Foundry agent: stay with text parts, since only text is supported there","Files are small and the network round trip matters more than payload size: inline `raw` can win over `url`","A downstream system needs strict schemas: define the `data` shape in the skill description and test it"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where to put content","head":["Choice","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Artifact with parts","Deliverables and results","Short conversational text","Needs a task to hang off"],["Message with parts","Questions, scoping and status","Anything the client must keep","May not be persisted or replayed [[r1]]"],["`url` part","Large or protected files","No fetchable URL or short-lived access","Needs expiring authenticated links"],["`raw` part","Small inline files","Large content or streamed events","Base64 inflates payloads by about a third"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A client reconnects and the result it was waiting for is gone","cause":"The agent delivered the result only inside a status message, which can be missed after a disconnect","fix":"Return deliverables as artifacts and fetch them with `GetTask` after reconnecting [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Streaming slows and memory spikes on large outputs","cause":"Large files were sent as inline `raw` parts in events","fix":"Send a `url` part, or chunk artifacts with `append` and `lastChunk`"},{"symptom":"The receiver misreads structured results","cause":"A JSON string was placed in a `text` part without a media type","fix":"Use a `data` part with `mediaType: application/json` and document the shape"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t05-s1","title":"A 40 MB report from a long-running job","situation":"A remote agent produces a 40 MB PDF after a 15-minute job. Callers sometimes disconnect before it finishes. The file is confidential.","constraints":["Large, confidential file","Callers may disconnect","Result must be retrievable later"],"unknowns":["How long can the agent keep the file?","Can links be authenticated and expiring?"],"question":"How does the agent deliver the report?","options":[{"name":"A task artifact with a `url` part pointing to an authenticated, expiring link","verdict":"best","why":"The result survives disconnects, stays out of the event stream and access can be controlled.","tradeoff":"The agent must run file storage and issue links."},{"name":"A task artifact with an inline `raw` part","verdict":"good","why":"A single self-contained response with no extra fetch.","tradeoff":"A 40 MB file becomes about 53 MB of base64 in JSON."},{"name":"Stream the bytes in status messages","verdict":"poor","why":"Status messages may be missed on reconnect and are not for deliverables.","tradeoff":"Right only for transient progress text."}],"flips":[{"if":"The file is 50 KB","then":"Inline `raw` is fine."},{"if":"Callers cannot fetch URLs from the agent's domain","then":"Chunk it as an artifact over the stream with `append` and `lastChunk`."}],"takeaway":"Deliverables are artifacts, and big ones travel by reference."},{"id":"w09-t05-s2","title":"A structured quote consumed by code","situation":"An order-processing agent calls a supplier agent for quotes and parses the price, currency and lead time programmatically. Quotes must be reproducible in audits.","constraints":["Machine consumer","Audit needs reproducible results","Schema stability required"],"unknowns":["Does the supplier version its output schema?","Is a human summary also needed?"],"question":"How should the supplier return the quote?","options":[{"name":"An artifact with a `data` part (`application/json`) and an optional text part for humans","verdict":"best","why":"Typed fields for code, a retained artifact for audits and a readable summary alongside.","tradeoff":"Both sides must agree on and version the JSON shape."},{"name":"JSON inside a `text` part","verdict":"good","why":"Works with text-only peers and needs no extra modality.","tradeoff":"Parsing is fragile and the media type does not say it is JSON."},{"name":"A status message sentence the caller parses with an LLM","verdict":"poor","why":"Non-deterministic parsing of a message that may not be persisted.","tradeoff":"Fine for a human chat summary, not for money."}],"flips":[{"if":"The peer is Foundry and supports only text","then":"Use JSON in a text part with a documented shape."},{"if":"Only a human reads the quote","then":"A text part is enough."}],"takeaway":"Machines get `data`, humans get `text`."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Where should an agent return the deliverable of a task?","options":["In a status message on the task","In the Agent Card","As an artifact of the task","In the `metadata` of the next request"],"answer":2,"explain":"The spec says results SHOULD be artifacts and messages are for communication. Status messages may not be persisted or replayed, the card describes the agent and request metadata is not a result channel."},{"id":"w09-t05-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"A `Part` holds exactly one of which content fields? Select all that apply.","options":["`text`","`raw`","`artifactId`","`url`"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The content fields are `text`, `raw`, `url` and `data`. `artifactId` identifies an artifact and is not a part field."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the difference between a Message and an Artifact in A2A?","back":"Messages carry communication: questions, scoping and status. Artifacts carry task deliverables. Results SHOULD be artifacts."},{"front":"Which four content fields can a Part use?","back":"Exactly one of `text`, `raw`, `url` or `data`, plus `mediaType`, `filename` and `metadata`."},{"front":"What do `append` and `lastChunk` do on an artifact update?","back":"`append` extends an earlier artifact with the same id. `lastChunk` marks the final chunk, so large artifacts can stream."}],"related":["w09-t04","w09-t06","w09-l03","w13-t01"],"refs":["r1","r17","tr2"]},{"id":"w09-t06","order":6,"group":"Protocol","title":"Operations and protocol bindings","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A2A defines one set of operations (send, stream, get, list, cancel, subscribe, push config, extended card) and exposes them through three functionally equivalent bindings: JSON-RPC, gRPC and HTTP+JSON.","useIf":"You implement or call an A2A endpoint and must pick a binding or debug a call across stacks.","avoidIf":"You hand-roll the wire format when an SDK exists: the SDK handles method names, headers and streaming for the binding you choose.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The spec defines a canonical data model in Protocol Buffers and requires every binding to provide functionally equivalent behaviour [[r1]]. The operations are `SendMessage`, `SendStreamingMessage`, `GetTask`, `ListTasks`, `CancelTask`, `SubscribeToTask`, four push-notification config operations and `GetExtendedAgentCard`. `SendMessage` returns a Task or a direct Message."},{"t":"table","caption":"Operation mapping [[r1]]","head":["Operation","JSON-RPC and gRPC method","HTTP+JSON endpoint"],"rows":[["Send a message","`SendMessage`","`POST /message:send`"],["Send and stream","`SendStreamingMessage`","`POST /message:stream`"],["Get a task","`GetTask`","`GET /tasks/{id}`"],["List tasks (cursor pagination, scoped to the caller)","`ListTasks`","`GET /tasks`"],["Cancel a task","`CancelTask`","`POST /tasks/{id}:cancel`"],["Reattach to a stream","`SubscribeToTask`","`POST /tasks/{id}:subscribe`"],["Extended card","`GetExtendedAgentCard`","`GET /extendedAgentCard`"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**JSON-RPC 2.0** runs over HTTP (`application/json`, PascalCase method names). **HTTP+JSON** is REST style and SHOULD use the media type `application/a2a+json`. **gRPC** uses the proto service. Streaming is Server-Sent Events for JSON-RPC and HTTP+JSON and native streams for gRPC. Two service parameters travel as HTTP headers (or gRPC metadata): `A2A-Version` (`Major.Minor`) and `A2A-Extensions`. An unsupported version returns `VersionNotSupportedError`, and an empty header means 0.3 [[r1]] [[r4]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Which binding you can use is set by the card's ordered `supportedInterfaces` (first is preferred) and by platform limits. Foundry's incoming A2A for v1.0 supports **JSON-RPC only**, text only and no streaming (checked 2026-10-13) [[r17]]. The Agent Framework .NET client prefers HTTP+JSON over JSON-RPC by default and lets you override it with `A2AClientOptions.PreferredBindings` [[r15]]. A `tenant` field lets one endpoint host many agents."}],"whereToUse":["Choosing a binding when you publish an agent for browsers, mobile apps or other services","Calling a Foundry-hosted agent, which accepts only JSON-RPC for v1.0","Debugging a failed call: matching the method or endpoint, version header and media type","Internal high-throughput agent meshes that already run gRPC","Gateway rules that route by path, such as `/message:send`, or by method name"],"whenToUse":{"use":["JSON-RPC for the widest SDK and platform support, including Foundry","HTTP+JSON when clients are browsers or REST tooling and you want resource-style URLs","gRPC for internal service meshes with typed contracts and native streaming"],"avoid":["Do not assume every peer supports every binding: read the card","Do not omit `A2A-Version`: servers may default to 0.3","Do not build a custom binding unless you can document equivalent error mappings"],"flips":["The peer is Foundry for v1.0: JSON-RPC is the only option","A browser must call the agent directly: HTTP+JSON with SSE is the natural fit","Payload throughput dominates inside one cluster: gRPC reduces overhead"]},"alternatives":{"title":"The three bindings","head":["Binding","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP","Broad support, simple clients, Foundry peers","You want REST resources and cacheable GETs","Everything is a POST to one URL"],["HTTP+JSON","Browsers, REST gateways, resource URLs","The peer implements only JSON-RPC","You must confirm the peer lists this binding in its card"],["gRPC","Typed internal meshes, native streaming","Browsers cannot call native gRPC directly","Tooling and firewall changes"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A client built for v0.3 cannot parse replies from a v1.0 agent","cause":"v1.0 uses SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE enums, drops the `kind` discriminator and renames operations (`message/send` became `SendMessage`) [[r2]]","fix":"Send `A2A-Version`, read `protocolVersion` per interface and support both versions during migration"},{"symptom":"A client picks HTTP+JSON but the server only serves JSON-RPC","cause":"The client preferred a binding by default instead of choosing from the card's interfaces","fix":"Select from `supportedInterfaces` and set preferred bindings explicitly [[r15]]"},{"symptom":"Foundry returns 400 with a version-ambiguous problem","cause":"The `A2A-Version` header and the `a2a-version` query value disagreed","fix":"Send one selector, or make both equal. With none, Foundry serves 0.3 [[r17]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t06-s1","title":"Calling a Foundry-hosted agent","situation":"Your orchestrator must call an agent hosted in Foundry Agent Service using A2A v1.0. The orchestrator's SDK defaults to HTTP+JSON and wants streaming for a chat UI.","constraints":["Callee is Foundry, v1.0","Orchestrator prefers HTTP+JSON","UI wants streaming"],"unknowns":["Can the UI accept non-streaming replies?","Does the SDK allow preferred bindings?"],"question":"What do you configure?","options":[{"name":"JSON-RPC, non-streaming, `A2A-Version: 1.0`, Entra token","verdict":"best","why":"It matches what Foundry supports for v1.0: JSON-RPC, text, no streaming.","tradeoff":"The UI cannot stream tokens from this peer."},{"name":"HTTP+JSON with streaming","verdict":"poor","why":"Foundry v1.0 does not support HTTP+JSON, gRPC or streaming.","tradeoff":"Right for peers whose card lists those bindings."},{"name":"Put a streaming facade in front of polling","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps the UI responsive while the call stays JSON-RPC.","tradeoff":"More moving parts and still no token streaming."}],"flips":[{"if":"Foundry later declares streaming for v1.0 on its card","then":"Enable SSE after reading the card."},{"if":"The peer is not Foundry","then":"Choose the binding from its card."}],"takeaway":"The callee's declared bindings beat the client's default."},{"id":"w09-t06-s2","title":"Internal agent mesh at high volume","situation":"Forty internal agents exchange hundreds of requests per second inside one cluster that already runs gRPC with mutual TLS. Streaming progress is common.","constraints":["Same cluster and platform team","High request rate","gRPC tooling available"],"unknowns":["Do all agent SDKs support gRPC?","Do any browsers call these agents directly?"],"question":"Which binding do you standardize on?","options":[{"name":"gRPC with native streaming","verdict":"best","why":"Typed contracts and lower overhead fit an internal mesh that already runs gRPC.","tradeoff":"Browsers need a gateway and some SDKs may lack gRPC."},{"name":"JSON-RPC over HTTP","verdict":"good","why":"Works everywhere and is easier to debug with curl.","tradeoff":"Higher serialization overhead and SSE instead of native streams."},{"name":"A custom binding","verdict":"poor","why":"It must define equivalent error mappings and nobody else supports it.","tradeoff":"Only justified by a transport the three standard bindings cannot use."}],"flips":[{"if":"External partners need to call the agents","then":"Offer JSON-RPC or HTTP+JSON at the edge."},{"if":"Some agent SDKs lack gRPC","then":"Standardize on JSON-RPC."}],"takeaway":"Pick the binding your platform and peers already run."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t06-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each HTTP+JSON endpoint to its operation.","pairs":[{"l":"`POST /message:send`","r":"Send a message"},{"l":"`POST /message:stream`","r":"Send and stream"},{"l":"`GET /tasks/{id}`","r":"Get a task"},{"l":"`POST /tasks/{id}:cancel`","r":"Cancel a task"}],"explain":"The endpoints map one to one to `SendMessage`, `SendStreamingMessage`, `GetTask` and `CancelTask`."},{"id":"w09-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A client sends no `A2A-Version` header to a server that supports 1.0 and 0.3. Which version does the spec say applies?","options":["1.0","0.3","The latest the client's SDK supports","The call is rejected with 401"],"answer":1,"explain":"An empty version header means 0.3. This is why clients should always send the version explicitly. Servers return `VersionNotSupportedError` for versions they do not support, and 401 is an authentication error."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which three bindings does A2A v1.0 define?","back":"JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP, gRPC and HTTP+JSON. All must be functionally equivalent."},{"front":"Which binding does a Foundry agent accept for incoming A2A v1.0?","back":"JSON-RPC only, with text modality and no streaming (checked 2026-10-13)."},{"front":"What do an empty `A2A-Version` header and an unsupported version mean?","back":"Empty means 0.3. An unsupported version returns `VersionNotSupportedError`."}],"related":["w09-t03","w09-t07","w09-l04","w09-t10","w11-t25"],"refs":["r1","r2","r4","r15","r17"]},{"id":"w09-t07","order":7,"group":"Interaction modes","title":"Streaming, polling and push notifications","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A2A offers three ways to hear about task progress: poll with GetTask, stream over Server-Sent Events, or receive webhook calls (push notifications). Task duration, caller reachability and update frequency decide.","useIf":"You call or build an agent whose work outlasts a single quick reply and you must choose how updates arrive.","avoidIf":"You use a blocking SendMessage for jobs that may run for minutes: the call waits until the task is terminal or interrupted.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"`SendMessage` is **blocking by default**: with `returnImmediately` false the call waits until the task reaches a terminal or interrupted state. Set `SendMessageConfiguration.returnImmediately` to true to get the task back at once and then use one of three mechanisms [[r1]]."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Polling** with `GetTask`: always available, works behind firewalls, simple, but adds latency and wasted requests. Best for infrequent updates [[r1]].","**Streaming** with `SendStreamingMessage` or `SubscribeToTask`: low latency over an open connection, needs `capabilities.streaming`. The stream starts with a `Task` snapshot, delivers `TaskStatusUpdateEvent` and `TaskArtifactUpdateEvent` in order, and ends when the task ends. If it drops, reconnect with `SubscribeToTask` [[r7]] [[r1]].","**Push notifications**: the client registers a webhook (`TaskPushNotificationConfig` with `url`, optional `token` and `authentication`). The agent POSTs a `StreamResponse` payload on significant changes. Needs `capabilities.pushNotifications`. The client typically then calls `GetTask` [[r7]]."]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-t07-d1","title":"Push notification flow","alt":"The client sends a message with a push configuration. The agent returns a submitted task. The agent later posts a status update to the client's webhook with credentials, and the client calls GetTask for the full task.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant A as Agent\n  participant W as Client webhook\n  C->>A: SendMessage with push config\n  A-->>C: Task SUBMITTED\n  A->>W: POST statusUpdate with credentials\n  W-->>A: 2xx\n  W->>A: GetTask taskId\n  A-->>W: Task with artifacts"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Webhooks need defences on both sides","md":"Agents SHOULD NOT post to any URL a client supplies, because attackers could aim them at internal services (SSRF) or use them as DDoS amplifiers: use allowlists, ownership checks and egress controls. Agents SHOULD reject private, loopback and link-local URLs, use 10 to 30 second timeouts and may retry with exponential backoff. The agent MUST authenticate to the webhook. The receiver MUST verify authenticity (for example JWT signatures checked against the agent's JWKS, or HMAC) and SHOULD guard against replay and process idempotently [[r7]] [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["A chat or dashboard UI that shows live progress of a task over an open connection","A serverless function or mobile client that cannot hold a connection for hours","A batch job behind a strict firewall that can only make outbound calls","Event-driven backends that react to a task finishing or asking for input","Gateways that must bound open connections and prefer short requests"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Stream when a connected client benefits from partial results or progress within seconds to minutes","Use push for hours-long tasks and callers that are reachable by HTTPS but cannot keep a connection open","Poll when firewalls block inbound calls, updates are rare or the peer supports neither streaming nor push"],"avoid":["Do not use blocking `SendMessage` for open-ended jobs","Do not register webhooks on endpoints you do not authenticate and verify","Do not assume a stream replays missed events: resume with `SubscribeToTask` and then `GetTask`"],"flips":["The peer is Foundry (no streaming for v1.0): fall back to polling [[r17]]","The caller sits behind a firewall with no inbound path: push is out, poll with backoff","Updates arrive every second and users watch: streaming beats polling on cost and latency"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Delivery mechanisms","head":["Mechanism","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Blocking `SendMessage`","Short jobs under a few seconds","Work is long or may pause for input","Connection and gateway timeouts"],["Polling (`GetTask`)","Infrequent updates, restrictive networks","Users expect live progress","Latency and wasted requests"],["Streaming (SSE or native)","Live progress and partial artifacts","Hours-long tasks, serverless callers","Open connections, reconnect logic"],["Push (webhook)","Long tasks, disconnected but reachable callers","No inbound path, or browsers","SSRF, authenticity, replay and idempotency work"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The caller hangs for the whole job on `SendMessage`","cause":"`returnImmediately` defaults to false, so the call blocks until terminal or interrupted","fix":"Set `returnImmediately: true` and then poll, stream or rely on push [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"A dropped stream loses the result","cause":"The client treated the stream as the only source of truth","fix":"Reconnect with `SubscribeToTask` (it starts with the current `Task`) and confirm with `GetTask` [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"The agent's webhook calls reach internal services","cause":"A client supplied a private or loopback URL and the agent posted to it blindly","fix":"Allowlist, verify ownership of the URL, block private ranges and use egress controls [[r7]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t07-s1","title":"A web UI with a 3-minute task","situation":"A browser UI shows progress for a task that runs about 3 minutes and emits an update every few seconds. Users stay on the page.","constraints":["Short-to-medium duration","Frequent updates","Connected browser client"],"unknowns":["Does the peer declare `streaming: true`?","How do gateways treat long connections?"],"question":"How should updates arrive?","options":[{"name":"Streaming over SSE with `SubscribeToTask` for reconnects","verdict":"best","why":"Low latency and incremental artifacts for a connected user.","tradeoff":"Needs reconnect logic and a gateway that tolerates open connections."},{"name":"Polling `GetTask` every 2 seconds","verdict":"good","why":"Works everywhere and survives proxies.","tradeoff":"Latency up to the interval and many wasted requests."},{"name":"Push to a webhook","verdict":"poor","why":"A browser has no stable HTTPS endpoint for the agent to call.","tradeoff":"Right for server-side callers that cannot stream."}],"flips":[{"if":"The peer does not declare streaming","then":"Poll with a short interval."},{"if":"The job stretches to hours","then":"Let a backend receive push and notify the UI."}],"takeaway":"Connected users and frequent updates mean streaming."},{"id":"w09-t07-s2","title":"A two-day task and a serverless caller","situation":"A serverless function starts a job that takes up to two days. It can expose a public HTTPS endpoint with token verification. Anyone supplying webhook URLs is a risk to the agent's side.","constraints":["Very long duration","Caller cannot hold connections","Both sides internet reachable"],"unknowns":["Does the agent declare `pushNotifications: true`?","Can the agent validate webhook URLs?"],"question":"How should updates arrive?","options":[{"name":"Push to an authenticated webhook, then `GetTask` on notification","verdict":"best","why":"No open connection for two days and the caller reacts only on state changes.","tradeoff":"You run a verified, idempotent receiver and the agent must defend against SSRF."},{"name":"Polling with exponential backoff","verdict":"good","why":"Needs no inbound endpoint and is always supported.","tradeoff":"Idle polls for two days and delayed reaction."},{"name":"Streaming for the whole duration","verdict":"poor","why":"A two-day connection will drop and a function cannot hold it.","tradeoff":"Right for minutes-long jobs with a connected client."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent does not declare push","then":"Poll with backoff."},{"if":"Webhook delivery is unreliable","then":"Pair push with a periodic `GetTask` sweep."}],"takeaway":"Hours or days with a reachable caller point to push, backed by polling."},{"id":"w09-t07-s3","title":"A batch job behind a strict firewall","situation":"A nightly batch job sits in a network zone with no inbound connections. It starts 200 tasks that each run about 10 minutes.","constraints":["No inbound traffic allowed","Medium-length tasks","Many tasks in parallel"],"unknowns":["Is there a rate limit at the agent?","Can outbound long-lived connections pass the proxy?"],"question":"How does the job learn each task finished?","options":[{"name":"Poll `GetTask` with jittered backoff","verdict":"best","why":"It needs only outbound HTTPS and is supported by every A2A server.","tradeoff":"Request volume grows with task count, so batch and back off."},{"name":"One stream per task","verdict":"good","why":"Low latency and works over outbound HTTPS if the proxy allows long connections.","tradeoff":"200 open connections and reconnect handling."},{"name":"Push to a webhook","verdict":"poor","why":"The zone accepts no inbound calls, so the agent cannot reach the job.","tradeoff":"Right if a relay endpoint outside the zone can receive and forward."}],"flips":[{"if":"A relay in a public zone is allowed","then":"Receive push there and forward events."},{"if":"Latency matters for each result","then":"Stream if the proxy tolerates it."}],"takeaway":"When nothing can call you, you call them."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A caller invokes `SendMessage` for a job that runs 25 minutes and the call hangs until a gateway times out. What was missed?","options":["`returnImmediately` defaults to false, so the call blocks until the task is terminal or interrupted","`SendMessage` is only valid for streaming agents","The task must first be subscribed to","A webhook must be registered before any send"],"answer":0,"explain":"Blocking is the default. Setting `returnImmediately` to true returns the task at once. `SendMessage` works for any agent, subscribing is optional and webhooks are one of three delivery options, not a prerequisite."},{"id":"w09-t07-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which controls belong on the agent or receiver side of push notifications? Select all that apply.","options":["Reject private, loopback and link-local webhook URLs","Authenticate the sender to the webhook and verify signatures or tokens","Trust any URL the client supplies to reduce latency","Process notifications idempotently and guard against replay"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The spec guidance covers SSRF defences, mutual authenticity checks, and replay and idempotency handling. Trusting any supplied URL is the vulnerability, not a control."},{"id":"w09-t07-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"The spec recommends webhook request timeouts of 10 to 30 seconds. What is the upper bound, in seconds?","answer":30,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"seconds","explain":"Agents SHOULD use a reasonable timeout for webhook requests, recommended at 10 to 30 seconds. They may retry with exponential backoff."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `SendMessageConfiguration.returnImmediately` do?","back":"If true, the task is returned right after creation. If false (default), the call waits until the task is terminal or interrupted."},{"front":"How do you resume a dropped A2A stream?","back":"Call `SubscribeToTask`. The stream begins with the current `Task` snapshot, then continues with ordered events."},{"front":"Which capability flags gate streaming and push?","back":"`capabilities.streaming` and `capabilities.pushNotifications` on the Agent Card. Without them the operations are unsupported."}],"related":["w09-t04","w09-t06","w09-t08","w09-l04","w16-t08"],"refs":["r1","r7","r17"]},{"id":"w09-t08","order":8,"group":"Interaction modes","title":"Long-running and input-required tasks","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Long A2A tasks can pause in INPUT_REQUIRED or AUTH_REQUIRED and resume when the client replies on the same task. Clients must persist ids, choose an update mode and plan for retention and restarts.","useIf":"A remote agent's job may run for minutes to days, ask clarifying questions or need credentials mid-way.","avoidIf":"Every call finishes in one reply: skip task persistence and interrupted-state handling.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"An interrupted state means the agent stopped for the client. In `TASK_STATE_INPUT_REQUIRED` it needs more input, from a human or from the client agent. In `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` it needs authorization. The client continues by sending a new message with the **same `taskId` and `contextId`**. Mismatched ids are rejected, and a message with only `taskId` lets the server infer the context [[r1]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-t08-d1","title":"An input-required round trip","alt":"The client sends a request and gets a working task. The agent later moves the task to input required with a question. The client gets an answer from a human and replies on the same task. The task completes with an artifact.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant H as Human\n  participant C as Client agent\n  participant A as Remote agent\n  C->>A: SendMessage returnImmediately\n  A-->>C: Task WORKING\n  A-->>C: Task INPUT_REQUIRED with question\n  C->>H: Ask the question\n  H-->>C: Answer\n  C->>A: Message with same taskId and contextId\n  A-->>C: Task COMPLETED with artifact"},{"t":"p","md":"For **in-task authorization** the agent uses a Task, moves it to `AUTH_REQUIRED` and explains what it needs. The credential should arrive out of band. The spec is explicit that agents MUST NOT treat the state transition itself as authorization, and a credential obtained in that state MUST NOT be assumed to authorize later messages unless an implementation, issuer or extension says so. A client that is itself an agent can pass the request up by moving its own task to `AUTH_REQUIRED` [[r1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Duration is an operations problem. Agents MAY expire contexts and SHOULD document the policy [[r1]]. Foundry keeps A2A tasks and contexts for 60 days from the most recent write, and each write resets the clock (checked 2026-10-13) [[r17]]. In Agent Framework, `A2AAgent` returns a continuation token when you pass `background=True` (Python) or `AllowBackgroundResponses` (.NET), supports `poll_task` or resubscribing, and stores `context_id`, `task_id` and `task_state` in the session so the next message continues an input-required task or references a finished one with `reference_task_ids` [[r15]]."}],"whereToUse":["Approval flows where a remote agent must ask a human before an irreversible step","Research or analysis agents that ask clarifying questions before spending hours","Agents that need user-delegated OAuth consent partway through a task","Orchestrators that persist task ids so work survives a restart of the caller","Nested delegation chains where an inner agent escalates a question outward"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Persist `taskId`, `contextId` and the last state wherever the client can restart","Reply to `INPUT_REQUIRED` on the same task to keep the agent's progress and context","Choose polling, streaming or push by duration, and always be able to fall back to `GetTask`"],"avoid":["Do not auto-answer questions on consequential decisions without a written policy","Do not treat an `AUTH_REQUIRED` credential as blanket authorization for the rest of the task","Do not rely on a stream staying open across a long human delay"],"flips":["Retention at the remote agent is shorter than the human delay: start a new task with the answer included","A policy answers the question: let the client agent reply automatically and log it","The credential would pass through several agents: negotiate an out-of-band or extension-based exchange bound to the requesting agent"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to handle a pause","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Reply on the same task","Context and progress matter","Retention expired or the agent restarted without state","Client must persist ids and handle retention"],["Cancel and start a new task","Retention is short or state is cheap","The task did expensive work already","Loses partial work, may repeat cost"],["Auto-answer by policy","Low-risk, well-defined questions","Decisions with financial or safety impact","Wrong answers go unnoticed without logging"],["Escalate to the caller's own user","A human must decide","No human is reachable in time","Chains of `INPUT_REQUIRED` across agents add delay"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"An answer to a clarifying question starts a fresh task","cause":"The reply omitted `taskId`, or used a new `contextId`, so the server treated it as a new request","fix":"Echo the `taskId` and `contextId` from the interrupted task [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"A destructive step ran after the client replied to `AUTH_REQUIRED`","cause":"The agent treated the state change or the credential as authorization for the operation","fix":"Define and check authorization for each sensitive operation; the state alone authorizes nothing [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Conversation context disappears after weeks or a restart","cause":"The remote agent expired the context or lost in-memory state, and the client stored no ids","fix":"Persist ids and state, design for expiry (Foundry: 60 days from last write) and fall back to a new task [[r17]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t08-s1","title":"A nested question inside a delegation chain","situation":"An orchestrator delegates a procurement task to a supplier agent. The supplier asks which cost centre to use. The orchestrator's own user is a finance analyst working in a chat session.","constraints":["Interrupted task in a chain","Financial impact if wrong","A human is reachable within minutes"],"unknowns":["Does policy define a default cost centre?","How long does the supplier keep the task?"],"question":"How does the orchestrator respond?","options":[{"name":"Move its own task to `INPUT_REQUIRED`, ask the analyst and reply on the supplier's task","verdict":"best","why":"The decision stays with the person accountable and the supplier's progress is preserved.","tradeoff":"Adds human delay to the chain."},{"name":"Answer from the orchestrator's memory of past cost centres","verdict":"good","why":"Fast when the context makes the answer unambiguous.","tradeoff":"A stale or wrong inference posts to the wrong ledger."},{"name":"Pick the most common cost centre and continue","verdict":"poor","why":"A guess on a financial field defeats the reason the supplier asked.","tradeoff":"Fine only where a documented policy defines the default."}],"flips":[{"if":"A written policy defines the cost centre by department","then":"Reply automatically and log the policy used."},{"if":"The analyst is unreachable for days and retention is short","then":"Cancel and restart with the answer in the first message."}],"takeaway":"Escalate consequential questions to the person who owns the decision."},{"id":"w09-t08-s2","title":"The caller restarts in the middle of a six-hour task","situation":"A caller running on autoscaled containers starts a six-hour analysis task. Containers are replaced several times a day and no state survives in memory.","constraints":["Caller process is not durable","Very long task","Cost of repeating six hours is high"],"unknowns":["Can the caller store ids in a database?","Does the agent declare push or streaming?"],"question":"How does the caller keep track of the task?","options":[{"name":"Persist `taskId` and `contextId`, then reattach with `GetTask` or `SubscribeToTask` after each restart","verdict":"best","why":"The task lives at the agent, so a new container can pick up the same handle.","tradeoff":"A durable store and a recovery path to build."},{"name":"Block on a single `SendMessage` call","verdict":"poor","why":"A restart drops the connection and the caller loses its only handle.","tradeoff":"Right for jobs shorter than the container lifetime."},{"name":"Re-submit the request after every restart","verdict":"good","why":"Simple to code and works if the agent is idempotent.","tradeoff":"May start duplicate six-hour tasks and double the cost."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent supports push and the caller has a stable endpoint","then":"Receive completion by webhook and still keep ids for recovery."},{"if":"The task takes 20 seconds","then":"Blocking is fine."}],"takeaway":"The task handle belongs in durable storage, not in a process."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t08-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps of an input-required round trip in order.","items":["Client starts the task and gets a working task","Agent moves the task to `INPUT_REQUIRED` with a question","Client obtains the answer from a human or policy","Client replies with the same `taskId` and `contextId`","Agent resumes and completes with an artifact"],"explain":"The agent pauses the task, the client resolves the question outside the protocol and then replies on the same task so progress and context continue."},{"id":"w09-t08-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"A client supplies a credential while a task is in `AUTH_REQUIRED`. What does the spec say about later messages on that task?","options":["The credential is automatically valid for the rest of the task","It is valid only for streaming operations","It replaces the transport authentication","It MUST NOT be assumed to authorize later messages unless the implementation, issuer or an extension defines that"],"answer":3,"explain":"The spec says the state alone authorizes nothing and scope and validity are defined by the implementation, credential issuer or an extension. Transport authentication still applies to every request."},{"id":"w09-t08-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"On Foundry, a task was last written 50 days ago. A client sends a reply today. How long until it expires?","options":["10 days","Immediately, because it is older than 30 days","60 days from today, because each write resets retention","It never expires"],"answer":2,"explain":"Foundry retains A2A tasks and contexts for 60 days from the most recent write and each new write resets the period (checked 2026-10-13)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does a client resume an `INPUT_REQUIRED` task?","back":"By sending a new message with the same `taskId` and `contextId`. Mismatched ids are rejected."},{"front":"What does `AUTH_REQUIRED` authorize by itself?","back":"Nothing. Agents MUST NOT treat the state transition as authorization. Scope and validity come from the implementation, issuer or an extension."},{"front":"How long does Foundry retain A2A tasks and contexts?","back":"60 days from the most recent write. Each write resets the period (checked 2026-10-13)."},{"front":"How does Agent Framework surface a long A2A task?","back":"As a continuation token when background responses are enabled. Use `poll_task` or resubscribe, and session state keeps `context_id`, `task_id` and `task_state`."}],"related":["w09-t04","w09-t07","w09-t09","w10-t21","w10-t22","w11-t16","w11-t24"],"refs":["r1","r15","r17"]},{"id":"w09-t09","order":9,"group":"Security and platforms","title":"A2A authentication and trust","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A2A treats agents as ordinary enterprise HTTP services: identity lives in the transport, credentials are obtained out of band, the server authenticates every request and authorizes per skill, task and data.","useIf":"Your agent calls agents you do not own, or others call yours, and you must decide how identity, delegation and trust boundaries work.","avoidIf":"You assume the Agent Card or a task state grants trust: neither authenticates or authorizes anyone.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A2A payloads carry no identity. Authentication happens at the HTTP or transport layer. The card advertises schemes in `securitySchemes` (API key, HTTP, OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, mutual TLS), the client obtains credentials **out of band**, and the server MUST authenticate every request. Production traffic MUST use TLS. The spec recommends TLS 1.3 or newer, while the enterprise guide says TLS 1.2 or higher, so treat 1.3 as the target and 1.2 as the floor [[r1]] [[r8]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"How A2A layers trust [[r1]] [[r8]]","head":["Layer","Rule","Design consequence"],"rows":[["Transport","TLS mandatory in production, clients verify the server certificate","Terminate TLS only at hops you control"],["Authentication","Every request, credentials in headers, 401 with `WWW-Authenticate`","No identity or tokens inside message parts"],["Authorization","Server decides by skill, action, data policy and scope; 403 when denied","Per-skill scopes and least privilege"],["Task scope","Operations are scoped to the caller, not-found hides other callers' tasks","Return `TaskNotFoundError` for foreign ids"],["In-task authorization","`AUTH_REQUIRED` hands a credential job to the client and authorizes nothing by itself","Check authorization per sensitive operation"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Skills can carry their own `securityRequirements`, so `place-order` may need a scope that `stock-check` does not. OAuth flows in the spec are `authorizationCode`, `clientCredentials` and `deviceCode`, with `implicit` and `password` marked deprecated. The v1.0 migration page says those two were removed, which conflicts with the spec. The spec is authoritative [[r1]] [[r2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Delegated identity** is a design choice, not a protocol feature. A client-credentials token proves which application called. If the callee must act for a particular user, use a user-bound flow such as authorization code and keep the user identity in the token, not in message text. On Foundry, incoming A2A requires a Microsoft Entra token and the **Foundry Agent Consumer** role (or another role granting endpoint access); assign it to the identity's object (principal) ID, not its client ID. Key-based and anonymous access are not supported (checked 2026-10-13) [[r17]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"In-task credentials can travel","md":"If a credential moves in band through a chain of agents, every agent in the chain sees it. The spec recommends credentials bound to the originating agent and readable only by it, and prefers secure out-of-band delivery [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["Securing a partner-facing agent with per-skill OAuth scopes","Authorizing a Foundry-hosted agent so only specific identities can call it","Reviewing how a client agent forwards user identity to a remote agent","Placing an API gateway in front of agents for rate limits, quotas and audit","Designing what a human must approve when a task asks for credentials"],"whenToUse":{"use":["OAuth 2.0 client credentials for application-to-application calls with scopes per skill","OAuth authorization code (or an on-behalf-of exchange) when the callee must act as a user","Mutual TLS or private networking for a small set of high-trust internal peers"],"avoid":["Do not place user or client identity inside message parts","Do not use one shared API key for many partners or for sensitive skills","Do not let the card, a skill description or a task state stand in for authorization"],"flips":["The callee must enforce per-user access to data: move from application tokens to user-bound tokens","A partner cannot do OAuth: an API key per partner behind a gateway with rate limits is a fallback","Data is regulated: sign cards, pin hosts and log task ids and trace context on both sides"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Security schemes","head":["Scheme","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["API key","Simple partner integrations behind a gateway","Many callers or sensitive skills","Shared secrets, no user identity"],["OAuth 2.0 client credentials","App-to-app, scopes per skill","The callee must know the end user","Token issuer and scope design"],["OAuth 2.0 authorization code","Acting on behalf of a user","Unattended batch work","Consent flows, token refresh, delegation chains"],["OpenID Connect","You already federate identity","Machine callers without an identity provider","Provider dependency and discovery config"],["Mutual TLS","A few high-trust peers, private networks","Many external partners","Certificate issuance and rotation"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A valid Entra token gets 401 or 403 from a Foundry agent over A2A","cause":"The calling identity lacks the Foundry Agent Consumer role, or the role was assigned to the client ID instead of the object ID","fix":"Assign the role to the principal (object) ID at the project or agent scope [[r17]]"},{"symptom":"Users see tasks that belong to someone else","cause":"Task lookups were not scoped to the authenticated caller","fix":"Scope `GetTask`, `ListTasks` and `CancelTask` to the caller and return not found for foreign ids [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"A credential travelled through three agents and was reused","cause":"An in-band credential was forwarded unbound","fix":"Prefer out-of-band delivery and bind credentials to the requesting agent [[r1]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t09-s1","title":"Many partners calling your quoting agent","situation":"Fifty partners call your agent. Reading quotes should be open to all of them, but placing orders is limited to contracted partners. Auditors want per-partner logs.","constraints":["External callers at scale","Different skills need different rights","Per-partner audit"],"unknowns":["Can partners run OAuth clients?","Is a gateway available for rate limits?"],"question":"Which scheme do you declare on the card?","options":[{"name":"OAuth 2.0 client credentials with scopes per skill, behind a gateway","verdict":"best","why":"Scopes map to skills, tokens identify each partner and the gateway adds rate limits and logs.","tradeoff":"Partners need OAuth clients and you operate an issuer."},{"name":"Mutual TLS per partner","verdict":"good","why":"Strong identity at the transport layer.","tradeoff":"Certificate issuance and rotation for fifty partners, and coarse authorization."},{"name":"One shared API key","verdict":"poor","why":"No per-partner identity, no per-skill rights and one leak exposes everyone.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a prototype with one trusted partner."}],"flips":[{"if":"Partners are few and trusted","then":"Mutual TLS is simpler."},{"if":"Partners cannot run OAuth","then":"Issue one API key each, behind a gateway with quotas."}],"takeaway":"Map scopes to skills and identify each caller."},{"id":"w09-t09-s2","title":"A remote agent that reads a user's mailbox","situation":"A remote agent must summarize one user's mailbox when that user asks. A wrong identity would expose another person's mail.","constraints":["Acts on behalf of an end user","Highly sensitive data","Per-user access control"],"unknowns":["Does the callee support user-bound tokens?","How is consent captured and revoked?"],"question":"How does the callee learn who the user is?","options":[{"name":"A user-bound token (authorization code or on-behalf-of) validated by the callee","verdict":"best","why":"Identity is cryptographically bound to the request and the callee enforces the user's own access.","tradeoff":"Consent flows, token refresh and delegation-chain design."},{"name":"An application token plus the user id in the message text","verdict":"poor","why":"A2A payloads carry no identity, so any caller could claim any user.","tradeoff":"Fine only where the callee has no per-user data."},{"name":"A service account that reads every mailbox, filtered afterwards","verdict":"good","why":"Simple to build.","tradeoff":"Over-privileged: one flaw exposes everyone's mail."}],"flips":[{"if":"Work runs unattended overnight","then":"Use a scoped app identity with explicit, audited access grants."},{"if":"The callee only needs the user's group","then":"A signed claim in the token may suffice."}],"takeaway":"Per-user access needs a user-bound credential, never a name in the payload."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Where does A2A carry the caller's identity?","options":["In a `userId` field of the message","In the transport layer credentials, such as HTTP headers","In the Agent Card of the caller","In the `contextId`"],"answer":1,"explain":"A2A payloads carry no identity. Credentials travel in standard headers or transport metadata and the server authenticates every request. Message fields, cards and context ids can be forged or are unrelated."},{"id":"w09-t09-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which statements about A2A security are correct? Select all that apply.","options":["The server MUST authenticate every incoming request","Moving a task to `AUTH_REQUIRED` authorizes the operation that follows","Authorization can be applied per skill using scopes","Production traffic MUST use TLS"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Every request is authenticated, authorization may use per-skill scopes and production needs TLS. The `AUTH_REQUIRED` state authorizes nothing by itself."},{"id":"w09-t09-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A caller with a valid Entra token gets denied by a Foundry agent over A2A. Which check comes first?","options":["Switch the binding to gRPC","Add an API key header","Disable TLS verification","Confirm the identity holds the Foundry Agent Consumer role, assigned to its object ID"],"answer":3,"explain":"Foundry requires Entra auth and a role that grants endpoint access, assigned to the principal's object ID. Foundry v1.0 supports JSON-RPC only, does not accept keys and TLS must stay on."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why does A2A put no identity in the message payload?","back":"Identity is established at the transport layer with standard HTTP credentials, so payload fields can be forged and are never trusted for auth."},{"front":"What does `TASK_STATE_AUTH_REQUIRED` authorize on its own?","back":"Nothing. It asks the client to obtain a credential, and scope and validity come from the implementation, issuer or an extension."},{"front":"What does a caller need to reach a Foundry agent over A2A?","back":"A Microsoft Entra token and the Foundry Agent Consumer role (or a role granting endpoint access), assigned to the object ID. No keys or anonymous access."}],"related":["w09-t03","w09-t08","w09-t10","w09-l05","w08-t11","w12-t15","w12-t16"],"refs":["r1","r2","r8","r17"]},{"id":"w09-t10","order":10,"group":"Security and platforms","title":"A2A in Agent Framework and Foundry","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Microsoft Agent Framework and Foundry Agent Service can both call and expose A2A agents. Foundry's a2a tool and A2A 1.0 are generally available, while the Agent Framework A2A packages are prerelease, and each has limits worth knowing.","useIf":"You build on Microsoft's stack and need agents to call, or be called by, agents on other platforms.","avoidIf":"Your agents share one process and team: Agent Framework's agent-as-tool pattern is simpler and cheaper.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Calling** a remote agent: Agent Framework's `A2AAgent` wraps any A2A endpoint as a normal agent, so `run` and streaming work as usual. You can resolve a card from a well-known URI (`A2ACardResolver`), start from a card you got from a registry, or configure the endpoint directly. The .NET client prefers HTTP+JSON over JSON-RPC unless you set `PreferredBindings`. A long task returns a continuation token you can poll or resubscribe to, and the A2A `context_id`, `task_id` and `task_state` live in the session [[r15]]. `A2AAgent` is only a transport wrapper: the remote agent's tools stay invisible and are changed on the remote side [[r15]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Exposing** an agent: .NET uses `Microsoft.Agents.AI.Hosting.A2A.AspNetCore` (`AddA2AServer`, `MapA2AHttpJson`, `MapWellKnownAgentCard`). Python uses `A2AExecutor` from `agent-framework-a2a`, built on `a2a-sdk`, which maps agent output to A2A events and artifacts and passes the A2A `context_id` as the session id. Go uses `a2aprovider`. You author and publish the Agent Card yourself. Install the packages with `--prerelease` (.NET) or `--pre` (Python): they are prerelease as of 2026-10-13 [[r14]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Status and limits as of 2026-10-13","head":["Capability","Foundry Agent Service","Agent Framework"],"rows":[["Call a remote A2A agent","`a2a` tool (A2A 1.0, GA) through a project connection. `a2a_preview` (0.3) is Preview [[r16]]","`A2AAgent` client, prerelease [[r15]]"],["Expose your agent over A2A","Incoming A2A for prompt agents: v1.0, JSON-RPC only, text only, no streaming, Entra auth only [[r17]]","ASP.NET Core, Python or Go hosting, prerelease. Bindings and streaming are yours to configure [[r14]]"],["Versions","Serves 0.3 unless you send `A2A-Version: 1.0` or fetch the v1.0 card [[r17]]","Python `agent-framework-a2a` moved to `a2a-sdk` v1.0 in python-1.4.0 (2026-05-14) [[tr4]]"],["Auth","Connection types include key, OAuth, Entra token, managed identity and agentic identity [[r16]]","`AuthInterceptor` on the Python client [[r15]]"],["Retired","The Connected Agents tool is not in the new Foundry Agent Service [[r16]]","Agent as function tool stays the in-process option [[r13]]"]]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w09-t10-d1","title":"A Foundry agent calling a remote A2A agent","alt":"A user talks to a Foundry agent. The agent uses the a2a tool, which reads a project connection that stores authentication, and calls the remote A2A agent. The Foundry agent uses the reply to answer the user.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> F[Foundry agent]\n  F --> T[a2a tool]\n  T --> C[Project connection with auth]\n  C --> R[Remote A2A agent]\n  R --> F"},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Mixed maturity, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Foundry's `a2a` tool and A2A 1.0 are GA. `a2a_preview` and A2A 0.3 are Preview. The Agent Framework A2A packages need prerelease flags. Say the label and the date whenever you recommend one [[r14]] [[r16]]."}],"whereToUse":["A .NET or Python orchestrator that calls a partner's agent through `A2AAgent`","A Foundry prompt agent that delegates to a remote specialist through the `a2a` tool and a project connection","A Foundry prompt agent exposed to other teams as a text-only A2A endpoint","A self-hosted Agent Framework agent behind ASP.NET Core or Starlette that other platforms call","Migration of classic Connected Agents setups to the A2A tool or workflows"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The `a2a` tool for new Foundry integrations that target A2A 1.0","Foundry incoming A2A when callers need text Q and A and Entra auth is acceptable","Self-hosted Agent Framework A2A servers when you need streaming, other bindings or non-text artifacts"],"avoid":["Do not start new work on `a2a_preview` unless you must stay on 0.3","Do not expect `A2AAgent` to expose or configure the remote agent's tools","Do not ship prerelease A2A packages unpinned"],"flips":["Callers need streaming or file artifacts from your Foundry agent: self-host an A2A server instead","A partner only speaks 0.3: send the 0.3 version explicitly and plan the move to 1.0","Both agents are yours and in one process: use agent-as-tool and skip A2A"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to connect agents on Microsoft's stack","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Agent Framework `A2AAgent`","Code-first orchestrator calling any A2A agent","You want a managed, portal-configured connection","Prerelease packages, you own auth and retries"],["Foundry `a2a` tool with a project connection","Foundry agents calling remote A2A agents with stored credentials","The peer needs non-text content over a Foundry target","Text-only and no streaming for Foundry targets [[r17]]"],["Foundry workflows","Orchestrating several Foundry agents declaratively","Peers outside Foundry","Tied to Foundry's workflow model [[r16]]"],["Agent as function tool (Agent Framework)","Agents in one process and team","Cross-team or cross-organization boundaries","Coupling, shared release cycle [[r13]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Replies arrive in the 0.3 shape from a Foundry agent","cause":"No `A2A-Version` header or `a2a-version` query value was sent, so Foundry served 0.3","fix":"Send `A2A-Version: 1.0`, or let the client fetch the v1.0 card so the SDK negotiates [[r17]]"},{"symptom":"Package install fails or resolves an old version","cause":"The A2A packages are prerelease and need `--prerelease` (.NET) or `--pre` (Python)","fix":"Pass the flag and pin the version in your lock file [[r14]]"},{"symptom":"Conversation context vanishes after a restart","cause":"The session with `context_id`, `task_id` and `task_state` was not persisted, or the remote agent expired it","fix":"Persist the session (`to_dict`) and design for retention limits (Foundry keeps 60 days from last write) [[r15]] [[r17]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w09-t10-s1","title":"A .NET orchestrator calling a partner and a Foundry agent","situation":"A .NET orchestrator must call a partner's Python agent and a team's Foundry prompt agent. Both publish A2A cards. The partner's job may run for 20 minutes.","constraints":["Code-first orchestrator","One long-running remote agent","Mixed platforms"],"unknowns":["Which A2A versions do the peers serve?","How do the peers authenticate callers?"],"question":"How does the orchestrator call them?","options":[{"name":"`A2AAgent` per peer, resolved from cards, with background responses for the long job","verdict":"best","why":"One programming model for both peers, continuation tokens for the 20-minute task and card-driven interface choice.","tradeoff":"Prerelease packages and your own auth handling per peer."},{"name":"REST function tools written per peer","verdict":"good","why":"Full control and no prerelease dependency.","tradeoff":"You rebuild tasks, context ids and polling for the long job."},{"name":"The Connected Agents tool","verdict":"poor","why":"It is not available in the new Foundry Agent Service.","tradeoff":"Right only for legacy classic agents that still run."}],"flips":[{"if":"The orchestrator is itself a Foundry agent","then":"Use the `a2a` tool with a project connection."},{"if":"Package prerelease status is unacceptable","then":"Use plain REST tools and accept the extra code."}],"takeaway":"Use the SDK that models tasks and context ids for you."},{"id":"w09-t10-s2","title":"Exposing an agent that streams and returns files","situation":"Another team wants live progress and a generated PDF from your agent over A2A. Your agent currently runs as a Foundry prompt agent.","constraints":["Callers need streaming and file artifacts","Foundry incoming A2A is text-only and non-streaming","Moderate traffic"],"unknowns":["Can callers accept polling and a link in text?","Is a prerelease package acceptable?"],"question":"How do you expose it?","options":[{"name":"Self-host an Agent Framework A2A server with streaming and artifacts","verdict":"best","why":"You control bindings, streaming and artifact parts, which Foundry's incoming endpoint does not offer for v1.0.","tradeoff":"You run hosting, auth and scaling, and depend on prerelease packages."},{"name":"Foundry incoming A2A with polling and a download link in text","verdict":"good","why":"Managed endpoint with Entra auth and no hosting work.","tradeoff":"No live progress and files only by reference."},{"name":"Foundry incoming A2A with files base64-encoded into text","verdict":"poor","why":"Large payloads in text break limits and clients cannot treat them as files.","tradeoff":"Tolerable only for tiny snippets."}],"flips":[{"if":"Callers only need a text answer","then":"Use Foundry incoming A2A."},{"if":"Foundry later declares streaming for v1.0 on its card","then":"Re-evaluate and move back to the managed endpoint."}],"takeaway":"Match the host to the modalities and streaming you must support."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w09-t10-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which Foundry A2A tool type targets A2A 1.0 and is generally available?","options":["`a2a_preview`","`a2a`","`connected_agent`","`mcp`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`a2a` targets 1.0 and is GA, `a2a_preview` targets 0.3 and is Preview, and Connected Agents is not in the new Foundry Agent Service. `mcp` is a different protocol."},{"id":"w09-t10-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each item to its platform behaviour.","pairs":[{"l":"Foundry incoming A2A v1.0","r":"JSON-RPC only, text only, no streaming"},{"l":"`A2AAgent` tool visibility","r":"Remote agent's tools stay hidden"},{"l":"No A2A-Version sent to Foundry","r":"Served as 0.3"},{"l":"Agent Framework A2A packages","r":"Need prerelease install flags"}],"explain":"These are the four platform facts checked on 2026-10-13: Foundry's v1.0 limits, the transport-only wrapper, the 0.3 default and the prerelease status."},{"id":"w09-t10-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A team calls a Foundry agent over A2A without any version selector and the reply shapes look like the old protocol. What explains it?","options":["The agent was deployed with the wrong model","gRPC was negotiated by mistake","The Agent Card was unsigned","Foundry serves A2A 0.3 when no version is selected"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without an `A2A-Version` header or `a2a-version` query value Foundry serves preview 0.3. The model, binding and card signature do not decide the protocol version."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which Foundry A2A tool type is GA and for which protocol version?","back":"`a2a` for A2A 1.0 (GA). `a2a_preview` is for 0.3 and is Preview."},{"front":"What are the limits of Foundry's incoming A2A for v1.0?","back":"JSON-RPC only, text modality only, no streaming, Entra auth only, tasks and contexts kept 60 days from last write."},{"front":"How do you install the Agent Framework A2A packages?","back":"`--prerelease` for .NET packages and `--pre` for `agent-framework-a2a` in Python. They are prerelease as of 2026-10-13."},{"front":"What does `A2AAgent` hide from you?","back":"The remote agent's tools and reasoning. It is a transport wrapper, so tool changes happen on the remote side."}],"related":["w09-t02","w09-t06","w09-t09","w09-l05","w11-t25","w12-t11","w12-t08"],"refs":["r13","r14","r15","r16","r17","tr4"]}]});
